hadiths[612]{hadithnumber,text}:
"764","Sayyiduna Awf bin Malik Ashjajai (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) instructed us to anoint the soles of the feet on the occasion of the Battle of Tabuk, for a traveler for three days and three nights and for a resident for one day and one night."
"765","Ubada bin Nasa'i, on the authority of Abi, who is Ibn Amara, narrates that the Prophet, may God's prayers and peace be upon him, prayed in the house of Sayyidina Amara facing both Qiblas. He said, \O Messenger of Allah"
"766","Sayyidna Uqbah bin Aamir (RA) narrates that he attended the service of Sayyidna Umar (RA) on the occasion of the conquest of Damascus. He says that I was wearing socks. Sayyiduna Umar asked, \O Uqbah! How many days have you not removed your socks?\"" So I remembered what I wore from last Friday to this Friday. I said"
"767","The same hadith is also narrated along with another chain of transmission, but it contains the words that you have acted according to the Sunnah. There is no mention of anyone else in the chain of transmission between Yazid and the narrator named Ali."
"768","Umar bin Ishaq narrates that I read in the book of Ata bin Yasar, which was with Ata bin Yasar. He says: I asked Sayyida Maimunah (RA), who was the wife of the Prophet (PBUH), about wiping over the socks. So he said that I said, \O Messenger of Allah"
"769","Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that if the rules of the religion of Allah were in accordance with opinion, the lower part of the socks would have more right to be wiped than the upper part, but I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiping the upper part. The words of the tradition are from a narrator named Ibn Mukhlid."
"770","Abd Khair narrates that Sayyiduna Ali (RA) said to me that I believe that the lower part of the socks is more righteous than the upper part, but I saw the Prophet (PBUH) wiping the upper part."
"771","Sayyiduna Imran bin Hussain (RA) narrates that once we were traveling with the Prophet (PBUH) until morning approached. So the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered us to camp. When the people caught their eye, they fell asleep, until the sun rose. The first to wake up was Sayyiduna Abu Bakr. No one would wake up the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from sleep until he himself woke up. When Sayyiduna Umar woke up, he saw that the sun had risen. So he sat on the head of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and began to say Takbeer in a loud voice, until the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) woke up. When you woke up, you saw that the sun had risen. So you left. You traveled a bit, until the sun shone brightly. So you stopped and led us in prayer. One of the attendees stayed away. He did not pray with us. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished the prayer, he inquired, \What has prevented you from praying with us?\"" He said"
"772","Al-Husayn ibn Isma‘il narrated to us: ‘Ali ibn Muslim narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: ‘Abbad ibn Rashid narrated to us: I heard Abu Raja’ al-‘Utaridi say: I heard ‘Imran ibn Husayn say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) traveled with us one night, then we halted and did not wake up except by the heat of the sun. Six of us woke up—I have forgotten their names—then Abu Bakr (ra) woke up and began preventing them from waking the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), saying: “Perhaps Allah has detained him for a need of His.” Abu Bakr kept saying the takbir abundantly until the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) woke up. They said: “O Messenger of Allah, our prayer has gone [its time has passed].” The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “Your prayer has not gone. Depart from this place.” So they departed and traveled a short distance, then he alighted and prayed. He said: “Indeed, Allah has completed your prayer.” They said: “O Messenger of Allah, so-and-so did not pray with us.” He said to him: “What prevented you from praying?” He said: “O Messenger of Allah, I was struck by janabah.” He said: “Perform tayammum with clean earth and pray; then when you are able to use water, perform ghusl.” The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent ‘Ali in search of water. Each of us had a waterskin like the two ears of a rabbit, tucked between his skin and his garment. Whenever the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) thirsted, we rushed to him with water. He went on until the day rose high and he found no water. Then a figure appeared. ‘Ali (ra) said: “Stay where you are until we see what this is.” It was a woman between two water-skins. They said: “O maidservant of Allah, where is the water?” She said: “By Allah, there is no water for you; I drew water yesterday, then I traveled my day and night together, and we have reached this hour.” They said: “Go to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).” She said: “And who is the Messenger of Allah?” They said: “Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).” She said: “The madman of Quraysh?” They said: “He is not a madman, but he is the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).” She said: “O people, let me go, for by Allah I have left small children of mine in a flock, and I feared I would not reach them before some of them died of thirst.” They did not let her go at all until they brought her to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). He ordered the camel to be made to kneel, then untied the water-skin from its top, called for a large vessel, filled it with water, and handed it to the junub, saying: “Go and perform ghusl.” They said: “By Allah, we left no waterskin, no water-bag, and no vessel but we filled it with water while she watched.” Then he tied the water-skin from its top, sent off the camel, and said: “O woman, here is your water; by Allah, if Allah had not increased it, not a drop of your water would have been diminished.” He called for a cloak, it was spread out, then he said to us: “Whoever has something, let him bring it.” Men began bringing a shoe-lace, a piece of cloth, a handful of barley, a handful of dates, a piece of bread, until they gathered that for her. He tied it up for her, then asked her about her people and she informed him. She went until she came to her people. They said: “What detained you?” She said: “The madman of Quraysh seized me, and by Allah he is one of two men: either the greatest sorcerer between these two”—meaning the heaven and the earth—“or he is truly the Messenger of Allah.” Then the horsemen of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) began raiding those around them while they remained safe. The woman said to her people: “O my people, by Allah I see this man only as one who has thanked you for what he took of your water. Do you not see that he raids those around you while you are safe, not raided? Would you like something good?” They said: “What is it?” She said: “We go to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and submit.” So she came driving thirty households until they pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and entered Islam."
"773","Sayyidna Imran bin Hussain (RA) narrates that we were on a journey with the Prophet (PBUH). We walked all night. When the last part of the night happened, we fell asleep. There is nothing more enjoyable than sleeping at this time. The heat of the sun woke us up. He has quoted the previous hadith. The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) inquired, \O such and such a person! Did you not pray with us?\"" He said"
"774","Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) says about such a person who is traveling, if he has Janabat and does not have water with him, due to which he is afraid of being thirsty."
"775","It is narrated about Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar that once the funeral was brought, he was not in the state of ablution at that time. He performed Tayammum and offered the funeral prayer."
"776","Al-Khajri bin Zayd narrates that Sayyidna Zayd bin Thabit (RA) had the disease of dropping urine and he used to pray while the drops were coming out."
"777","Sayyidna Al-Khajri bin Zayd narrates that Sayyidna Zayd bin Thabit (RA) said Takbeer, then drops of his urine started coming out. They tried to hold back as much as they could, but when he overcame them, they performed ablution and prayed."
"778","Saeed bin Musayyib says that if it is flowing on my knees, then I will not finish the prayer. A narrator named Sufyan states that it refers to urine, i.e. when a person is suffering from an illness."
"779","Zubayd bin Salat narrates that I heard Sayyiduna Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) saying that when a person performs ablution and wears socks, he can pray over them by wiping them. If he wants, he should not take them off, but the ruling is different in the case of Janabat."
"780","This same hadith is narrated from the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the authority of Sayyidina Anas, along with another chain of transmission. A narrator named Ibn Sa'id states that according to my knowledge this hadith has been narrated only by Asad bin Musa."
"781","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Prophet (peace be upon him) said that when a person performs ablution and wears his socks, he can pray in them. He should anoint them, then if he wishes, he should not take them off, but in the case of Janabat, the ruling is different."
"782","Abd al-Rahman bin Abu Bakrah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates from his father that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) allowed the traveler to stay for three days and three nights, and the resident for one day and one night. When he performs ablution and wears socks, the permission is that he can wipe both socks between ablutions. This tradition is also narrated along with some other traditions."
"783","Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) says that if the rules of religion were related to opinion, the lower part of the sock would be more righteous than the upper part, but I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiping the upper part."
"784","Imam Zayd narrates the statement of Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding his father, his grandfather, that the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) instructed me to anoint the socks."
"785","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (RA) narrates that the Prophet (PBUH) used to wipe his bandage. This hadeeth is not authentic. Its narrator, Abu Amara, is very weak."
"786","Ibrahim narrates on the authority of Nakhai, Alqamah and Aswad that about a person who performs ablution and wipes his socks, then takes them off, they both said: \He will wash his feet.\"""""
"787","Sayyida Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates: Fatimah bint Abu Habish asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): \O Messenger of Allah! I am not pure"
"788","Sayyida Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates: Fatimah bint Abu Habish came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: \O Messenger of Allah! I am a woman suffering from istihazah. I am not pure. Shall I give up prayer?\"" He said: \""No"
"789","Sayyidna Fatima bint Abu Habish (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates that she was suffering from istihazah. The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: \When the menstrual blood comes"
"790","Sayyida Aisha Siddiqa Radiyallahu Anha narrates: Fatima bint Abu Habish was suffering from istihazah. The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: \Menstrual blood is black which is recognizable. When it is"
"791","Urwah narrates the statement of Sayyidah Fatimah bint Abu Habish (may God be pleased with her): She became afflicted with istihazah. The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: \When there is menstrual blood"
"792","Urwa narrates this statement about Sayyida Fatimah bint Abu Habish (RA): When she suffered from Istihazah, the Prophet (PBUH) said to her: \The menstrual blood will be black and it will be recognized"
"793","Sayyidah Umm Salama (RA) who is the wife of the Prophet (PBUH) narrates: Fatimah bint Abu Habish was a victim of Istihaha during the time of the Prophet (PBUH). Sayyida Umm Salma asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about this issue, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: \She should count the number of days she used to menstruate in the first months and give up the prayer during that time. When that time has passed"
"794","Salman bin Yasar narrates: Sayyidah Umm Salama (RA) asked the Prophet (PBUH) about the issue of Fatima bint Abu Habish, and the Prophet (PBUH) said: “She will abstain during the specified period of menstruation and after that she will perform ghusl and pray.” The same hadith is narrated from Sayyidah Umm Salama (RA) along with another chain of transmission, but it contains the words of the Prophet (PBUH): \She will wait for the days of her menses and abandon the prayer.\"""""
"795","Sulaiman bin Yasar narrates: Sayyida Fatimah bint Abu Habish (may Allah be pleased with her) complained of istihazah, until a large vessel was kept for her in which the blood would bleed. The narrator narrates: Then he said to Sayyidah Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her: \Ask the matter from the Holy Prophet"
"796","Sulaiman bin Yasar narrates: Sayyida Fatimah bint Abu Habish (RA) complained of istihazah, so she inquired from the Prophet (PBUH). The narrator doubts that these are the words: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the issue for her, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) instructed: “She should give up prayer during the specific days of her menstruation, and in addition, she should perform ghusl and perform the prayer after tying a cloth.” A narrator named Sulaiman was asked: \Did her husband have intercourse with her?\"" So they replied: \""We will narrate what we have heard.\"""""
"797","Ata states: \The maximum duration of menstruation is fifteen days.\"""""
"798","Ata states: \The maximum duration of menstruation is fifteen days.\"""""
"799","Ata states: \The maximum duration of menstruation is fifteen days.\"""""
"800","Ata states: \The maximum duration of menstruation is fifteen days.\"" Abu Ibrahim narrates: Imam Ahmad bin Hanbal adopted these two traditions and presented them as arguments."""
"801","Ata bin Abi Rabah states: The minimum period of menstruation is one day."
"802","The participant narrates that we had a woman who used to menstruate for fifteen days every month and that menstruation was perfect and normal."
"803","Uza'i narrates that we had a woman who used to menstruate in the morning and became pure in the evening."
"804","Hasan bin Saleh states that the maximum duration of menstruation is fifteen days."
"805","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Masoud (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say: Menstruation can last for (at least) three days or it can last for four or five or six or seven or eight or nine or ten days. This hadith has not been narrated by anyone except Haroon bin Ziyad along with this chain of authority, and this narrator is weak, and since it was transmitted from Amish, this tradition has no origin for the people of Kufa, and Allah knows best."
"806","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Menstruation may last for three days, or for four days, or for five days, or for six days, or for seven days, or for eight days, or for nine days, or for ten days."
"807","Sayyiduna Anas Radiyallahu Anhu narrates that menstruation can last three, four, five, six, seven, eight, nine, ten days."
"808","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say: The period of menstruation can be at least three days and at most ten days."
"809","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say: Menstruation cannot last more than ten days."
"810","Sufyan Thauri says: The minimum period of menstruation is three days and the maximum is ten days."
"811","Sayyiduna Anas says that a woman will be considered menstruating until the period lasts for ten days, if it lasts more than that, then she will be considered mustahad."
"812","Shaykh Abu Zareah Damaschi says: I saw Imam Ahmad bin Hanbal, he did not accept this tradition of Jald bin Ayyub."
"813","Hammad bin Zayd narrates that I and Jarir bin Hazim went to Jalad bin Ayyub and he narrated this tradition about a mustahadha woman, a mustahadha woman will wait for three days, five days or seven days or ten days, (that is, she will consider these days as menstruation), so we went and we tried to forbid them, but they did not differentiate between menstruation and istahadha."
"814","Sayyiduna Anas Radiyallahu Anhu used to say: A menstruating woman will wait for three, four, five, ten days, if the bleeding lasts more than ten days, then she will be Mustahadha, and she will take a bath and start praying."
"815","Uthman bin Abul-Aas narrates: A woman will not count as mustahadhah because of one or two or three days, until she reaches ten days."
"816","Uthman bin Abul As-Thaqafi states that a menstruating woman who continues to bleed after ten days, then she will be regarded as Mustahadha, she will perform ghusl and pray."
"817","Saeed bin Jubayr says: The maximum duration of menstruation is thirteen days."
"818","Sayyida Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates that Fatima bint Abu Habish came to the service of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and she said, “O Messenger of Allah, I am a woman suffering from istihadah.” The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, “This is blood from someone else’s vein. A narrator named Ammar is unique in narrating this tradition, it is weak, while according to the Muhadditheen, this tradition is suspended as it was narrated from Ismail (it contains these words) A woman suffering from istihaza will count the days of her menstruation, after which she will bathe and perform ablution for each prayer."
"819","Sayyida Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates that Fatima bint Abu Habish came to the service of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said, “O Messenger of Allah, I am suffering from Istihazah, so how can I be pure?” It is portable. Some narrators have narrated it as a marfu' tradition, while some narrators have narrated it as a suspended tradition, and these traditions are also authentic."
"820","Sayyida Aisha Siddiqa narrates that an Ansari woman came to the service of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and she said, \O Messenger of Allah"
"821","Sayyida Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates that Fatima bint Abu Habish came to the service of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, “It is blood from another vein."
"822","Sayyida Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates that Fatima bint Abu Habish attended the service of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, “You should abandon the prayer during the days of your menstruation, then take a ghusl and start praying even if drops of blood fall on the mat. There are these words in a hadith: Do you perform ablution for every prayer?"
"823","This tradition is narrated along with another chain of transmission, however, it contains these words of the Holy Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace: take a bath again, then perform ablution for each prayer, and perform the prayer, even though drops of blood are falling on the mat."
"824","Sayyida Aisha Siddiqa narrates that Fatimah bint Abu Habish came to the service of the Holy Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace."
"825","Sayyida Ayesha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates the saying of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): A musthahaza woman will pray even though drops of blood are falling on the mat."
"826","Abd al-Rahman bin Bishr narrates: We got up from Abdullah bin Dawud and came to Yahya bin Saeed, and he asked, \Where did you come from?\"" He replied"
"827","Imam Abu Dawud says that the hadith transmitted from Amish indicates that it is weak, that is, the narrator named Hafs narrated it from Amish as a fixed tradition and denied this. And there are these words in it that woman used to bathe before every prayer. All this statement is from Imam Abu Dawood."
"828","Sayyida Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) says: A mustahadhah woman will pray even if her blood drops are falling on the mat. The narrator named Hafs did not narrate this hadith as a valid tradition, while the narrator named Usama followed it."
"829","It is narrated about Sayyida Ayesha Siddiqa that she was asked about a musthahaza woman and she said that she will not give up prayer even if drops of blood are falling on the mat."
"830","It is narrated about Sayyida Ayesha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) that she was asked about a musthahaza woman, and she said, \She will not abandon the prayer"
"831","Yahya bin Moin says that a narrator named Habib bin Thabit has narrated two hadiths regarding Arwa, but both of them have no status."
"832","Sayyida Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates that Fatimah bint Abu Habish came and said: I have a complaint of istihaza, I am not pure. The woman said, \I am suffering from istihazah. My blood does not stop. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said"
"833","Sayyida Ayesha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) used to say: Recitation means purity."
"834","Sayyida Hamna bint Jahsh (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates: I was suffering from severe istihadah, I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to find out about it. This thing has stopped me from praying, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, “Put a piece of cloth, the bleeding will stop.” The woman said, “It is more.” The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said, “Tie a bandage.” He said, “It is more than that. If you have the power of both, then you know better. The Prophet, peace and blessings of God be upon him, said, \This is Satan's trick. According to the knowledge of God"
"835","This tradition is narrated along with another document."
"836","This tradition is narrated along with another document."
"837","This tradition is narrated along with another document."
"838","This tradition is narrated along with another document."
"839","Sayyida Asma bint Umays (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates, I said, O Messenger of Allah, Fatimah bint Abu Habish has been complaining of istihaza for so long. It is from the devil, she should sit in a large pot. When the woman sat on it, the yellowness of her period prevailed, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, \She should perform a ghusl once for Zuhr and Asr"
"840","Sayyida Asma bint Umays (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates, “I said, O Messenger of Allah, Fatimah bint Abu Habish has not prayed for such a long time. He should hasten Isha and pray for both of them after performing ghusl once."
"841","Ibn Abi Malika narrates: Sayyida Fatimah bint Abi Habish (may Allah be pleased with her) complained of Istihaza. She did not pray for such a long time. She came to the service of Sayya Ayesha (may Allah be pleased with her) and said: O Umm al-Mu'minin, I am afraid that I may not end up in hell and have no part in Islam. It has been a long time since I did not pray. I am bleeding Istihaza. said: You stay when the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to inquire from him in the way that the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had mentioned to me. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, “Tell Fatimah that she should give up prayer during the specific days of her menstruation in every month. When those specific days are over, she should take a bath once and put cotton on her private parts and tie a wide bandage around it so that (the blood does not come out) then perform ablution for each prayer and perform the prayer. There is a disease that he has contracted. This tradition is narrated along with another document."
"842","Ibn Abi Malika narrates that my aunt, Sayyida Fatimah bint Abu Habish, may Allah be pleased with her, came to the service of Sayyida Ayesha Siddiqa and said: I am afraid that I will not go to Hell, because I have not prayed for two years (the narrator doubts that these words are the words), Sayyida Ayesha said, \You wait when the Prophet"
"843","Sayyidah Umm Salama (RA) narrates that a woman asked the Prophet (PBUH) \I am suffering from impurity"
"844","Sayyida Umm Salma narrates that a woman's blood was flowing profusely and it would not stop. Sayyida Umm Salma asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about it, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, \That woman will examine the specific days in which she used to menstruate. She will give up prayer for that number of days"
"845","Sayyidna Abu Umamah Bahli (RA) narrates that the Prophet (PBUH) said, “A young girl and a woman who is disappointed with menstruation will not menstruate for less than three days, and it will not last more than ten days. Menstrual blood is black and thick, in which redness prevails, while the blood of Istihaza is yellow and thin. This tradition is not proven. Its two narrators, Abdul Malik and Alaa, are both weak, while the narrator named Makhool is not proven."
"846","Sayyidna Abu Umamah Radiyallahu Anhu narrates that the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, said this, the amount of menstruation of a virgin girl, Yathiba, is at least three days and the maximum is ten days. If the blood comes out profusely during the prayer, then he should put a cotton cloth, if the blood is still coming out, then put another cloth or a bandage. The narrator of this tradition is Abdul Malik Majhool, while the narrator named Alaa is Alaa bin Kathir and it is weak in hadith, while the narrator named Makhool has not heard any hadith from Sayyidna Abu Umamah."
"847","Sayyidna Wathila Bin Asqaa (RA) narrates that the Prophet (PBUH) said that the minimum period of menstruation is three days and the maximum period is ten days. The narrator of this hadith is Hamad bin Minhal unknown while Muhammad bin Ahmed bin Anas is weak."
"848","Sayyidna Jabir bin Abdullah Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: Fatimah bin Qays asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the issue of istihaza woman, what should she do? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, \She should count the specific days of her menstruation"
"849","Ata narrates that when a pregnant woman sees blood, Sayyida Aisha said that she cannot menstruate, so she will perform ghusl and pray."
"850","Sayyidah Umm Atiya (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates that we women did not care about the discharge that was visible after initiation, which was yellowish or yellowish in color."
"851","Sayyida Ayesha Siddiqa likes that a woman suffering from Istihazah should have intercourse with her husband."
"852","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said this about nifas (the maximum period is forty days, but if the woman observes tahr before that (then the ruling will be different). This hadith was narrated by a narrator named Hameed only by a narrator named Salam. This salam is long and it is weak in the hadith."
"853","Sayyidna Uthman bin Abul-Aas (RA) said to his wives: You used to not adorn yourself in front of me before forty days have passed. Do not exceed forty days."
"854","Sayyidna Uthman bin Abul As, may Allah be pleased with her, narrates that when she was purified from nifas, she adorned herself, then Sayyidna Uthman bin Abul As said, \Didn't I tell you?\"" The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) instructed us to stay away from pregnant women for forty days. A narrator named Umar bin Haroon has narrated it as a valid tradition while a narrator named Wakiy has narrated it contrary to it."""
"855","Sayyidna Uthman bin Abul As said to his wives: When a woman among you was in labor, she would not come near me for forty days. Similarly, this hadith has been narrated on the authority of Sayyiduna Umar (may God be pleased with him), Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas (may God be pleased with him), Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (may God be pleased with him) and others as their own sayings."
"856","Sayyiduna Uthman bin Abul-Aas (RA) says that the Prophet (PBUH) has fixed the period of forty days for pregnant women."
"857","The same hadith has been narrated by the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding Sayyida Ayesha Siddiqa. One of the narrators of this hadith is Abu Bilal Al-Ash'ari, while the other narrator is Ata bin Ajlan, the hadeeth is obsolete."
"858","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Amr (RA) narrated: The Prophet (PBUH) said that a woman who has given birth will wait for forty days. The narrators of this hadith are Amr bin Husain and Ibn Alashah, both weak and outdated."
"859","It is narrated about ``Aiz bin Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) that his wife suffered from nifas. After twenty days, she was purified and came to his bed. He said: What is your problem? He said: I have become pure, so he struck the woman with his foot and said: Get away from me, you are not something that can separate me from my religion until forty days have passed (you cannot come near me). In a hadith, these words are Sayyidna Ayaz bin Amr, may Allah be pleased with him: he is the person who pledged allegiance to the hand of the Holy Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, under the tree. This hadith has been narrated only by a narrator named Muawiya with reference to Jalad bin Ayyub and this narrator is weak."
"860","Sayyiduna Umar (RA) says that a woman in childbirth will sit for forty days."
"861","Sayyidna Mu'adh bin Jabal (RA) narrates the saying of the Holy Prophet (SAW) when seven days have passed for a postpartum woman and then she observes tahr, then she should perform ghusl and start praying. Its narrator Saleem narrates: Later, I met Ali bin Ali Muhaddith, he narrated the same tradition with reference to Sayyidna Muadh bin Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding Aswad, Ubadah, and Abd al-Rahman. The black narrator of this tradition is Ibn Thaalba the Syrian."
"862","Sayyida Umm Salma (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates that during the time of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), pregnant women used to sit for forty days."
"863","The same hadith is narrated along with another chain of transmission in it is al-Faat. She used to sit after her nifas."
"864","Abdullah bin Muhammad bin Abdul Aziz narrates that Imam Ahmad bin Hanbal was asked, I was listening to this, he was asked about the women who have given birth, if they see blood, how long should they sit, and he replied: For forty days, after that she will perform ghusl."
"865","Abdullah bin Abu Malikah narrates that Sayyida Ayesha Siddiqa was asked about the women who are pregnant, and she said that the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, was asked about it, and he said that she would stay for forty days, then she would perform ghusl and purify herself and start offering prayers. The narrator of this tradition is Ata, a obsolete hadith."
"866","Sayyida Umm Salama (RA) narrates this about the Prophet (PBUH): She asked the Prophet (PBUH) how long a woman would sit after giving birth to a child. So the Prophet said, \Such a woman will remain seated for forty days. If she observes tahr before that"
"867","Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) says that when a postpartum woman observes tahr, it is not permissible for her to abandon prayer."
"868","Abd al-Rahman bin Ghanum narrates: I asked Sayyiduna Mu'adh bin Jabal, may God be pleased with him, about a menstruating woman who becomes pure a little before sunset, and he said: \She will perform Asr prayer.\"" I asked: \""She will perform Maghrib prayer before sunset.\"" He said"
"869","Sayyidna Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: Once we accompanied the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Battle of Zaat al-Raqqa. (The judge quoted these words) When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) encamped at a place, he said, “Who will guard us tonight?” Then an Ansari and a Muhajir went forward. When they reached the end of the gorge, the Ansari man said to the Muhajir: In which part of the night do you dislike (the first part or the last part)? He said: In the first part, the narrator narrates that the emigrant fell asleep and the Ansari stood up and prayed. The same person (polytheist) came and when he saw the face of a person, he knew that it was for the protection of the people, so he shot that Ansari with an arrow that hit him. The person shot the third arrow which hit the Ansari, so the Ansari put it away, then the Ansari bowed, then went into prostration, then he lifted his companion (and said to him) get up because I am bleeding profusely. He had shot you earlier. Why did you not tell me then? This Ansari said: I was reciting such a surah and it did not please me to leave it in the middle before I finished it, but when this person kept shooting at me, then I went into Ruku (after finishing the prayer), then I woke you up. By Allah, what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had instructed me to protect, if I was about to waste it, (I had this desire) before finishing it. dying"
"870","Sayyidna Masoor bin Mukharama (RA) narrated: Sayyidna Umar (RA) continued to pray while his wound was bleeding."
"871","The same hadith is also related to Sayyidna Masoor bin Mukharama along with another chain of transmission about Sayyidna Umar (RA)."
"872","Syedna Jarhad (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him. He was in the mosque at that time. He was wearing a cloak in which his thigh was visible."
"873","This tradition is also narrated along with a document."
"874","Sayyiduna Ali bin Abu Talib (RA) narrated: The Prophet (PBUH) said to me that you should wear a veil over your thighs, because the thighs are part of a veil."
"875","Sayyiduna Ali bin Abu Talib (RA) narrated: The Prophet (PBUH) said to me: Do not uncover your thighs and do not look at the thighs of any living or dead person."
"876","Sayyiduna Ali bin Abu Talib (RA) narrated: I asked the Prophet (PBUH) about the bandage that is tied on a wound, etc. How can such a person perform ablution? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, \Such a person will be anointed with water in the state of janabat.\"" Then the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He will wipe his whole body"
"877","This same hadith along with another chain of transmission has been narrated from the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the authority of Sayyidna Ali bin Abu Talib (RA)."
"878","Imam Zaidi, may Allah be pleased with him, narrates this statement of Sayyiduna Ali bin Abu Talib regarding his father (Imam Zain al-Abidin) and his grandfather (Imam Hussain, may Allah be pleased with him). I have broken a joint."
"879","This tradition is narrated by Amr bin Khalid Wasti along with another chain of transmission."
"880","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates this on the authority of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a place where filth etc. is thrown."
"881","Narrated by Nafi, Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (RA) was asked about a place where dirt is thrown, can one perform prayer there? So he said, \When you water it three times"
"882","Sayyida Asma Bint Abu Bakr said this to Hajjaj Bin Yusuf: Once the Holy Prophet (Sallallahu Alaihi Wasallam) applied to his back, he gave his blood to my son, and my son drank it. So he said, \I don't like it that I should shed your blood.\"" The Prophet said"
"883","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said, \Every noble work that does not begin with the praise of Allah is incomplete. The narrator named Qara is unique in narrating this hadith with reference to Zuhri. The narrator narrates. Other narrators have described it as a transmitted tradition with reference to Zuhri"
"884","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: The Holy Prophet (SAW) said: Every important work that does not begin with the mention of Allah (SWT) is incomplete."
"885","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: A person asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) how many prayers Allah has made obligatory on His servants. The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Five prayers, he inquired: Have you performed any fard before and after them? The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah has made five prayers obligatory on His servants, so this person swore by Allah that He will not add anything to them, and He will not decrease anything from them."
"886","Abd al-Malik bin Rabi narrates this as a hadith on the authority of his father on the authority of his grandfather on the authority of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: When your children reach the age of seven, separate their beds; when they reach the age of ten, beat them for (skipping) prayer."
"887","Amr bin Shuaib narrates this statement of his grandfather regarding his father: The Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, said this: Command your children to pray at the age of seven, and when they reach the age of ten, beat them for this reason (i.e. for abandoning the prayer) and separate their beds. Seventy to the knees."
"888","Amr bin Shuaib narrates this statement of his grandfather regarding his father: The Prophet, peace and blessings of God be upon him, said this: Command your children to pray at the age of seven and beat them at the age of ten and separate their beds."
"889","Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The knee is a part of seventy. The narrator of this hadith is Abul-Junub: Weak."
"890","Sayyidna Ayyub Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: The part above the knees is seventy and the part below the navel is seventy."
"891","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Command them to pray at the age of seven and beat them at the age of thirteen for (skipping the prayer)."
"892","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said this: I have been commanded to fight with people until they bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and that Muhammad (PBUH) is the Messenger of Allah, and that they establish prayer and give Zakat, then their blood and wealth become forbidden to me, and they will be accountable to Allah. The same hadith is narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the authority of Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (RA) with another chain of transmission, while it is also narrated on the authority of Sayyiduna Anas (RA) on the authority of Sayyiduna Abu Bakr (RA) on the authority of the Prophet (PBUH)."
"893","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have been commanded to fight with the polytheists until they testify that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad (peace be upon him) is the Messenger of Allah. If we eat the slaughtered animals, their property and blood will become forbidden to us, but their right will remain intact: they will get everything that a Muslim gets, everything that is required of a Muslim will be required of them."
"894","The same hadith has been narrated from the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with reference to Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) along with another chain of transmission."
"895","This same hadith along with another chain of transmission has been narrated from the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the authority of Anas bin Malik."
"896","This same hadith along with another chain of transmission has been narrated from the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the authority of Anas bin Malik."
"897","This tradition is narrated along with another document."
"898","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates the saying of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I have been ordered to fight with the people until they bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and they establish prayer and pay zakat."
"899","This tradition is narrated along with another document."
"900","Sayyiduna Mu'adh bin Jabal (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said: I have been ordered to fight the people until they establish prayer and pay Zakat and bear witness."
"901","Abdullah Bin Mahreez narrates: This Sahib was under the care of Sayyidna Abu Mahzoora Radiyallahu Anhu, when he was sent to Syria, he used to say: I said to Sayyidna Abu Mahzoora Radiyallahu Anhu: Uncle, I am going to go to Syria, I am afraid that I will not be able to ask you about your call to prayer. I went out with some people. We were on the way to Hunain. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was returning from Hunain. Lia: You sent someone to take us until we were brought and stood before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). All the people pointed towards me: they said rightly: So the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left them all and stopped me. I got up at that time and there was nothing more distasteful to me than what the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had given me. I stood in front of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar, Ashdaan, Laala illa Allah, Ashhad in Laala illa Allah, Ashhad in Muhammad Rasool Allah, Ashhad in Muhammad Rasool Allah. Then he said to me: Read it again! And spread your voice then you said to me: Now read this: Ashdaan Lailaha illa Allah Ashhad in Lailaha illa Allah Ashhad in Muhammad Rasulullah Ashhad in Muhammad Rasulullah Hai Ali Salat Hai Ali Salat Hai Ali Falah Hai Ali Falah Allah Akbar Allah Akbar La ilaha but Allah. When I finished the call to prayer, the Prophet (peace be upon him) called me and gave me a bag containing some silver. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed his blessed hand on the forehead of Sayyidina Abu Mahzoora, then moved his hand on his face, then moved it on his chest, then moved it on his liver until the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) moved his blessed hand to the navel of Sayyiduna Abu Mahzoora, then the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: May he bless you and shower blessings on you. I allow you to do this. At that time, whatever dislike I had for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disappeared and it was all transformed into love for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then I went to Sayyiduna Uttab bin Asaid, who was the governor appointed by the Prophet (peace be upon him), and I started giving the call to prayer under the order of the Prophet (peace be upon him). A narrator named Ibn Juraj has narrated this: I heard this hadith from a man from the descendants of Sayyiduna Abu Mahzoora Radiyallahu Anhu, in the same way that Ibn Muhairiz narrated this hadith to me."
"902","Rabi' states: Imam Shafi'i has said this: I found Ibrahim bin Abdul Aziz and he gave Azad in the same way as Ibn Muhairiz narrated. I think that he has described this Iqamah as the same tradition as he has described the incident of Azan."
"903","Sayyiduna Abu Mahzoora Radiyallahu Anhu narrates: The Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, went to Hunain. I followed him with ten men from Mecca. The person gave the adhan separately, I gave the adhan last, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You come forward! Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made me sit in front of him, then he put his hand on my forehead and prayed for me three times, then he said: Go and call the Adhan to the House of Allah. I said: How is that? Ya Rasulullah! So the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught me the way of calling the call to prayer, the way the call to prayer is given today (the words of the calling to call to prayer were): Allah is great, Allah is great, Allah is great. These words are used in the first call to prayer in the morning. \Salaat khair min sleep Salat khair min sleep\"". Sayyiduna Abu Mahzoora (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught me how to say the words of Iqamah twice (his words are: \""The height of the prayer"
"904","Sayyidna Abu Mahzoora Radiyallahu Anhu narrates: I went to Hunain along with ten young people with the Prophet PBUH. At that time, the Prophet PBUH was the most disliked person to us. When these people gave the call to prayer, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes! This is the person from whom I heard the call to prayer. You go and call the call to prayer for the people of Makkah and you say to Atab that the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has given this order: I call the call to prayer for the people of Makkah. Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar. These words are two times, Ashhad in laala illa Allah, you should read this twice, Ashhad in Muhammad Rasool Allah, Hai ali Salat, twice Hay Ali al Falah, twice Allah akbar, Allah akbar la ilaha illa Allah, when you give the first call to prayer in the morning, say: Salat khair min al-Num. The narrator narrates: Sayyiduna Abu Mahzoora (RA) did not cut the hair of his forehead and did not make alms there because the Prophet (PBUH) touched him."
"905","Sayyiduna Abu Mahzoora (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) taught these words for the call to prayer: Allah is great, Allah is great, Allah is great."
"906","Ammar bin Saad narrates the statement of his father Sayyidna Saad Saad al-Qurz: This is the call to prayer of Sayyidna Bilal Radiyallahu Anhu, which was ordered by the Holy Prophet. Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar, Ashhadan Lailaha illa Allah, Ashhad in Lailaha illa Allah, Ashhad in Muhammad Rasulullah, Ashhad in Muhammad Rasulullah, then he returns and says Ashhad in Lailaha illa Allah, Ashhad in Lailaha illa Allah, Ashhad in Muhammad Rasulullah, Ashhad in Muhammad Rasulullah, Hai Ali Salat, Hai Ali Salat Hai Ali Al Falah, Hai Ali Falah Allah Akbar, Allah Akbar, La ilaha but Allah. His words of Iqamah are: The words of Iqamah became one by one, similarly this sentence was also said once: Sayyidna Saad Radiyallahu Anhu says: The Holy Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, said to me: Saad! When you do not see Bilal with me, then give the adhan. The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned his blessed hand over his head and said: O Saad! May Allah bless you! When you do not see Bilal with me, give the call to prayer. The narrator used to say: When Syedna Bilal asked (the Caliph at that time) Sayyidna Umar bin Khattab (RA) for permission to go to Jihad, then Sayyidna Umar (RA) asked him: O Bilal, to whom should I entrust the call to prayer? He replied: Sa'd's, because he gave the call to prayer for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Quba'. Sayyiduna Umar called Sa'd and said: The task of giving the call to prayer is entrusted to you and it will be entrusted to your children after you. Sayyiduna Bilal (RA) used to take it and walk it in front of the Prophet (PBUH) and bury it in the Eid Gah, so Sayyidna Sa'd did the same."
"907","Ibrahim bin Abdulaziz used to say: I have found my grandfather Amjad, my father and other relatives saying: He used to recite Iqamah in these words. Allahu Akbar Allahu Akbar Ashhad in ilaaha illa Allah Ashhad in Muhammad Rasool Allah Hai Ali Salat Hai Ali Al-Falah Qud Qad Salawat Allahu Akbar la ilaha ila Allah."
"908","Ibrahim bin Abu Mahzourah narrates the following saying of the Prophet, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, on the authority of his father, on the authority of his grandfather: The Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, called Sayyiduna Abu Mahzourah and taught him how to give the Adhan and instructed him to give the Adhan in Makkah, in which Allah the Great twice and the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, instructed him to recite the words of Iqamah once."
"909","Sayyiduna Abu Mahzoora (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) taught him the nineteen words of Iqamah. The words of the Adhan are: Allah is great, Allah is great, Allah is great. Similarly, the words of Iqamah will be recited twice and will not be repeated."
"910","Sayyiduna Abu Mahzoora Radiyallahu Anhu narrates: When I was a young boy, I gave the Fajr call to the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace."
"911","Sayyidna Abdullah bin Umar (RA) narrates: When the Muslims came to Madinah, they used to gather together at the time of prayer. They were not called (regularly) for it. One day they discussed about this, and someone said: You guys should ring the bell like the bells of the Christians, and some of you should play the trumpets like the Jews. Sayyidna Umar (RA) said, \Why don't you send some people?\"" So that they would announce for the prayer"
"912","Abdul Malik narrates the saying of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding his father: The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: O Abu Mahzura, recite the words twice in the call to prayer for every prayer and say in the call to prayer in the morning: Prayer is better than sleep."
"913","Sayyiduna Abu Mahzoora (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) taught me the nineteen words of the Adhan."
"914","Sayyiduna Abu Mahzoora (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) instructed him to recite the words of Adhan in even numbers and the words of Iqamah in odd numbers."
"915","Sayyiduna Ali bin Abu Talib (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) instructed us to make the Adhan (call to prayer) stand still and say the words of Iqamah quickly."
"916","Abu Zubair, the muezzin of Bayt al-Maqdis, narrated: Sayyiduna Umar bin Al-Khattab (RA) came to us and said, \When you give the call to prayer"
"917","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) had a muezzin who used to give the Adhan quickly, so the Prophet (PBUH) said: The Adhan is the path of gentleness and ease."
"918","Sayyidna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: Sayyidna Abu Mahzoura (RA) was instructed: He should recite the words of Adhan in even numbers and the words of Iqamah in odd numbers, and turn to the right and left while saying Iqamah."
"919","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: During the time of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the words of Adhan were used twice and the words of Iqamah were used once."
"920","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: During the time of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the words of the Adhaan were recited twice and the words of the Iqamah once, however, when the muezzin recited Qad Qat-e-Salah, he recited it twice."
"921","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: Sayyiduna Bilal was given this order: He should recite the words of Adhan in even numbers and the words of Iqamah in odd numbers."
"922","Sayyiduna Anas Radiyallahu Anhu narrates: Sayyiduna Bilal Radiyallahu Anhu used to recite the words of Adhan twice and the words of Iqamah once, except for the height of salat."
"923","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: Sayyiduna Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) was ordered: He should recite the words of Adhan in even numbers and the words of Iqamah in odd numbers."
"924","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Sayyiduna Bilal: He should recite the words of Adhan in even numbers and the words of Iqamah in odd numbers."
"925","This tradition is narrated along with another document."
"926","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Sayyiduna Bilal to recite the words of Adhan in even numbers and the words of Iqamah in odd numbers."
"927","Sayyiduna Anas Radiyallahu Anhu narrates: Sayyiduna Bilal Radiyallahu Anhu used to recite the words of Adhan twice and the words of Iqamah once, but he used to recite the words of Qad Qawat Salat twice."
"928","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Sayyiduna Bilal: He should recite the words of Adhan twice and the words of Iqamah once."
"929","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said this: Whoever continues to give the Adhan for twelve years, Jannah will become obligatory for him, and sixty good deeds will be written for each Adhan, and thirty good deeds will be written for each Iqamah."
"930","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said this: Whoever continues to give the Adhan for twelve years, Jannah will become obligatory for him, and because of his giving the Adhan, sixty good deeds will be written in return for the Adhan and thirty good deeds will be written in lieu of standing."
"931","Sayyiduna Salama bin Akwa (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: During the time of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the words of Adhan were used twice and the words of Iqamah were used once."
"932","It has been narrated about Sayyiduna Salama bin Akwa (may Allah be pleased with him) that when he could not pray in congregation, he would give the call to prayer (to pray by himself) and say Iqamah (he would say the words of Iqamah twice)."
"933","Imam Muhammad bin Ali narrates this statement of Sayyidna Ali (RA): Sayyidna Jibraeel had revealed the words of Iqamah separately, and the Prophet (PBUH) declared it permissible to recite the words twice in the Adhan."
"934","Sayyiduna Abu Rafi' (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and I heard Sayyiduna Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) give the Adhan in front of the Prophet (may Allah bless him and grant him peace)."
"935","Muhammad bin Abdullah narrates: My father (Sayyidna Abdullah bin Zayd may Allah be pleased with him) narrated this hadith to me: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the bell (for prayer), I saw in a dream a man who came to me and taught me this. He recited the words of Adhan twice and the words of Iqamah once. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If Allah wills, this dream will come true. Stand with Bilal and teach Bilal what you have seen, because his voice is louder than yours. When Sayyiduna Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, heard this, he said: By Him Who sent you with the truth, I have also seen a dream similar to what he saw. The Prophet, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, said: Then by Allah Praise in every way is specific (which has enabled us)."
"936","Sayyiduna Abdullah Bin Zayd Radiyallahu Anhu narrates: During the time of the Holy Prophet (Sallallahu Alaihi Wasallam), the words Adhan and Iqamah were paired. The narrator of this tradition is Ibn Abi Laili, Qazi Muhammad bin Abd al-Rahman, who was considered weak in the knowledge of hadith, because his memory was weak. Similarly, it is not proven that Ibn Abi Laili heard the hadith from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar."
"937","Abd al-Rahman bin Abu Laili narrates: Sayyidna Mu'adh bin Jabal (RA) narrated this: An Ansari man Abdullah bin Zayd (RA) came to the Holy Prophet (SAW) and said: O Messenger of Allah! I saw in a dream that a person came down from the sky, he was covered with two cloaks, he landed on a wall of Medina, he said the words of Adhan twice, then he sat down, then he stood up, then he said those words twice. A narrator named Abu Bakr bin Ayyash has narrated this. It is similar to the way in which the call to prayer is given today. The Prophet, peace and blessings of God be upon him, said, \Teach this to Bilal.\"" Then Sayyiduna Umar"
"938","Aoun bin Abu Juhaifa narrates the statement of his father: Sayyiduna Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) gave the adhan before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in which he said the words twice and then said the Iqamah in the same way."
"939","Aun bin Abu Huhaifa narrates this on the authority of his father: Sayyiduna Bilal gave the call to prayer before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) whose words were two times and the words of Iqamah were also two times. A narrator named Abu Aoun has narrated these words: The words of the call to prayer were two times Iqamah as well."
"940","Ibrahim Nakhai narrates this statement of Aswad: Sayyiduna Bilal used to recite the words of Adhan twice and recited the words of Iqamah twice."
"941","It is narrated about Sayyiduna Bilal (RA): His call to prayer and his Iqamah used to be repeated twice."
"942","This tradition is narrated along with another document. Ramadi says: Sufyan did not listen to him."
"943","Abdullah bin Muhammad narrates this statement of his grandfather (Sayyidna Abdullah bin Zayd) on the authority of his father: When he saw in a dream the method of the call to prayer, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Sayyidna Bilal, and he gave the call to prayer."
"944","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Sunnah is that when the muezzin gives the Fajr call to prayer, he should say: Hai Ali al-Falah, followed by salat khair min num, twice, and then say: Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar, Lailaha illa Allaha."
"945","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: Taswaib will be in the morning prayer when the muezzin says in the Fajr call to prayer, Hai Ali al-Salawat, and Hai Ali al-Falah, then salat khair min num, he will say."
"946","Sayyidna Abdullah bin Umar (RA) narrates this about Sayyidna Umar (RA): He said to his Muzan: When you reach the Azan of Fajr, Hayy Ali As-Salat, then say: As-Salat Khair Min-Num, As-Salt Khair Min-Num."
"947","Abd al-Rahman bin Abu Laili narrates this statement of Sayyiduna Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him: The Holy Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, instructed me: I should say Tasweeb in the Fajr call to prayer, but he forbade me to say Tasweeb in the Isha call to prayer."
"948","Malik bin Dinar narrates: When the sons of Sayyiduna Mahzura finished giving the call to prayer, I followed them to Masjid Haram (the minaret) and I said to them: Tell me about the call to prayer that your father gave in front of the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace. \Ashhad in Laala ila Allah"
"949","Sayyiduna Abu Mahzoora (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) taught him these words of the Adhan. \Allahu Akbar Allahu Akbar Ashhad in laala ila Allah"
"950","Aswad narrates that Syedna Bilal's call to prayer used to end with these words: Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar, Laala illa Allah."
"951","Aswad narrates: The last words of Sayyiduna Bilal's Adhan were: Laala ila Allah."
"952","Aswad narrated this about Sayyiduna Bilal: At the end of Sayyiduna Bilal's Adhan, these words were used: Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar laalaala illa Allah."
"953","Aswad narrates: Sayyiduna Bilal used to say these words at the end of the adhan: Allahu Akbar Allahu Akbar Laala ila Allah."
"954","Narrated by Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar: Once Sayyiduna Bilal gave the call to prayer before dawn, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him: He should go back and announce three times: Beware, the servant has slept (i.e., he has made a mistake). He went back and said in a loud voice: Beware, the servant had slept. Another narrator has followed him, but this narrator is weak."
"955","Narrated by Nafi': Sayyiduna Umar's muezzin, whose name was Masruh, gave the call to prayer in the morning before he became righteous, then Sayyiduna Umar ordered him."
"956","Ayyub narrates: Once Sayyiduna Bilal gave the call to prayer at night. This tradition is transmitted."
"957","Hameed bin Hilal narrates: Once Sayyiduna Bilal gave the call to prayer at night, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him: He should go back to his place and proclaim loudly: Beware! Surely the servant had fallen asleep (i.e. he made a mistake) so he went back, Sayyiduna Bilal Radiyallahu Anhu said: I wish Bilal's mother had not given birth to him."
"958","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (RA) narrates: Once Sayyidna Bilal (RA) gave the call to prayer before dawn, and the Prophet (PBUH) got angry and ordered him to say: Verily, the servant had fallen asleep, and Bilal (RA) felt very sorry for him. In this hadith, the narrator named Aamir is delusional. The correct tradition is the one that has passed before him. It is narrated by the narrator named Ibn Harb on the authority of Nafi regarding the muezzin of Sayyiduna Umar."
"959","Sayyidna Anas (RA) narrates: Once Sayyidna Bilal (RA) gave the call to prayer before dawn, and the Prophet (PBUH) ordered him: Go back and announce: Verily, the servant was asleep. Narrator Abu Yusuf is unique in narrating this tradition."
"960","Qatadah narrates: Once Sayyiduna Bilal gave the call to prayer, his chain of transmission does not mention Sayyiduna Anas Radi Allahu Anhu."
"961","Hasan al-Basri narrates the statement of Sayyidna Anas bin Malik (RA): Once Sayyidna Bilal (RA) gave the call to prayer, then the Prophet (PBUH) ordered him to repeat it again, then Sayyidna Bilal (RA) climbed the minaret and he was saying, “I wish Bilal’s Maan would cry for him and his forehead became sweaty. He repeated, then when the morning dawned, he gave the call to prayer. The narrator of this tradition, Muhammad bin Qasim Asadi, is very weak."
"962","Muhammad bin Abdullah narrates the statement of his uncle Sayyidna Abdullah bin Zayd: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended to adopt different forms, but he had not yet adopted any of them. Bilal gave the call to prayer, Sayyiduna Abdullah narrates, I was looking at him, I wanted to give it myself, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, \You say Iqamah.\"""""
"963","The same hadith is narrated from Sayyidina Abdullah bin Zayd on the authority of Muhammad bin Abdullah bin Muhammad along with another chain of transmission."
"964","Sayyidna Abu Saeed Al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There are two days in which you should not fast and two hours in which you should not pray, because Christians and Jews seek (worship) at that time. is)."
"965","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: After the dawn (in Nawafil) only Durkaat (i.e. Fajr prayers) will be performed."
"966","Qasim bin Muhammad narrates: We attended the service of Sayyida Aisha Siddiqa before the morning prayer. One day we attended her service and she was performing the prayer at that time. We asked her: Which prayer is this? So they replied, I had slept at night, and could not pay some Nawafal, so I did not want to abandon them."
"967","Amr Shaibani narrates: The owner of this house told us this while pointing to the house of Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Masoud, but he did not mention his name: I asked the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, which deed is more virtuous. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: To perform the prayer at its initial time. I asked, then which one? You said, Jihad in the way of Allah, I inquired: Then which one? You said to treat parents well. (Sayyidna Abdullah Radiyallahu Anhu says) If I had asked you more, you would have given me more answers."
"968","Abu Amr Shaybani narrates this from a companion of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked which deed is more virtuous. So you said that the most virtuous deed is to perform the prayer at its time. A hadith has these words: The prayer is to be offered at its earliest time."
"969","Abu Amr Shaibani narrates: The owner of this house narrated this hadith to me. He means Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Masoud. He said: I asked the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace."
"970","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best deed is to perform the prayer at its earliest time."
"971","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) was asked which deed is more virtuous. So he said: To perform the prayer at its initial time. A group of Muhadditheen have narrated contrary to it."
"972","Sayyida Umm Farah (RA) narrates: She heard the Prophet (PBUH) saying: The most excellent deed in the sight of Allah is to perform the prayer at its earliest time."
"973","Sayyidah Umm Farooah narrates: I asked the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Which deed is more virtuous? So he said: To perform the prayer at its initial time."
"974","The same tradition is narrated along with another chain of transmission, but it also mentions this: Sayyidah Umm Farah (may God be pleased with her) is one of the women who pledged allegiance to the hand of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) under a tree."
"975","Sayyida Umm Farah (may Allah be pleased with her) is one of the young women who accepted Islam at the behest of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). She narrates: One day I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioning the deeds."
"976","Sayyidah Umm Farah (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked. I was listening to this."
"977","Sayyidah Umm Farah (RA) narrates: I heard the Prophet (PBUH) saying: The most beloved deed to Allah is to perform the prayer at its earliest time. These words of the tradition are from a narrator named Umari."
"978","Qasim bin Ghannam narrates this regarding the woman who accepted Islam on the hand of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked which deed is more virtuous/so he said: Faith in Allah. You said to perform the prayer at its time."
"979","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A person who performs the prayer within its time but does not perform it in its early time (the person who performed the prayer) is better for that person than his family and wealth."
"980","Sayyida Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never offered the last prayer during his life until he passed away, only twice."
"981","Sayyida Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never offered the last prayer until he passed away."
"982","Sayyida Ayesha Siddiqa narrates: I never saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performing any prayer at the last hour until he passed away."
"983","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (RA) narrated: The Prophet (PBUH) said: Performing the prayer at the beginning of the prayer is a cause of obtaining the pleasure of Allah, and performing the prayer at the last time is a cause of forgiveness from Allah."
"984","Sayyiduna Jarir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The first time of the prayer is the cause of Allah's approval and the last time is the cause of Allah's forgiveness."
"985","Ibrahim bin Abd al-Malik narrates this statement of his grandfather on the authority of his father: The Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: The first time (of prayer) is the pleasure of God, the middle time is the mercy of God, and the last time is the forgiveness of God."
"986","Ibn Shihab narrates: Once Umar bin Abdulaziz was sitting on the pulpit (i.e. giving a sermon) and he delayed the Asr prayer a little. Tawrah bin Zubair said: Sayyidna Jibraeel told the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the time of prayer. Umar bin Abdulaziz said to him: Try to understand what you are saying. Yes, they say: I heard Sayyiduna Abu Mas'ud Ansari (RA) narrating this. He said this. I heard the Prophet (PBUH) saying: Once Gabriel (PBUH) came down and told me about the time of prayer. I prayed with him, then I prayed (the next prayer) with him. (Sayyidna Abu Mas'ud Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates:) I remember this about the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he used to perform the Zuhr prayer when the sun was setting, but when it was hot, he used to postpone this prayer. Then he could reach Dhu al-Halifa before sunset. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to offer the Maghrib prayer when the sun had set. He used to offer the Isha prayer when the horizon darkened. (The narrator says: There are these words in a hadith) The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform the morning prayer sometimes in the dark and sometimes in the light, but after that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) started performing it in the dark and this was the norm until the death of the Prophet (peace be upon him)."
"987","The same hadith is narrated along with another document, however, it contains these words: When he prayed Asr, the sun was bright and high. If a person walked, after finishing the prayer, he could reach Dhul-Hulaifah, which was six miles away, and he could reach there before sunset. In this tradition, there are also these words: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform the morning prayer in the dark, then one day the Prophet (peace be upon him) used to perform it in the light as well (but after that he adopted the habit of performing it in the dark) again. He never performed this prayer in the light and this was his routine until the death of the Prophet (PBUH)."
"988","Dashim bin Abdullah Nakhai narrated: We were sitting with Sayyiduna Ali (RA) in the Grand Mosque, Kufa was special in those days, the muezzin came to him and said: Amir al-Mu’minin: It is time for Asr prayer. Sayyidna Ali (RA) said, “Sit down.” He sat down. After finishing, they came back and sat in the same place, where they were sitting earlier, then we got up on our knees and tried to check that the sun had not set. The narrator of this hadith, Ziyad bin Abdullah Nakhai, is unknown, only a narrator named Abbas has narrated the hadith regarding him."
"989","Abdul Wahid bin Nafi narrates, I entered the mosque of Madinah Munawwarah and the muezzin gave the Asr call. The narrator narrates that there was an old man sitting there. He reprimanded the muezzin and said: My father told me that the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, used to instruct me to perform this prayer with delay. He is the son of Sayyidna Rafi bin Khadij). Imam Darqutni (may Allah have mercy on him) said: These sons of Sayyidna Rafi are not strong. A narrator named Musa bin Ismail, narrating this hadith, has stated this: His surname was Abur Mah, similarly there is a difference of opinion about his name."
"990","It is narrated about Abd al-Rahman bin Rafi: Once the muezzin gave the adhan for Asr, he gave the adhan a little early, so Sayyidna Abd al-Rahman bin Rafi reprimanded him and said: May you be in trouble! My father (who was a Sahabi Rasool) has told me this: The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has ordered the people to delay the Asr prayer. This tradition is also narrated along with some other isnads, while the opposite is narrated from some other Companions, it proves that the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) instructed to perform the Asr prayer early."
"991","Abu Najashi narrates: Sayyidna Rafi' bin Khadij (RA) narrated this hadith to me. He says: We used to perform Asr prayer with the Prophet (PBUH) and then a camel would be sacrificed, its meat would be cut into ten parts and then not cooked, and before sunset we would eat the roasted meat. The name of the narrator named Abu Najashi is Atta bin Suhaib, he is trustworthy and famous, he was in the service of Sayyidna Rafi bin Khadij for six years, Ikramah bin Ammar, Imam Uza'i, Ayyub and others have narrated hadiths regarding him. The tradition narrated by this narrator, which is narrated by Sayyidna Rafi' bin Khadij, is better than the tradition, which is narrated by a narrator named Abdul Wahid, regarding the son of Sayyidna Rafi', and Allah knows best."
"992","Narrated ``Urwa: I heard Sayyidna Bashir bin Abu Mas'ud narrating: Sayyidna Abu Mas'ud Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated this about the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) offered the Asr prayer, the sun was bright and high."
"993","Sayyidna Rafi' bin Khadij (RA) narrates, The Prophet (PBUH) said this: Should I not tell you about the prayer of a hypocrite, he keeps postponing Asr prayer until he performs it like a cow's face is slaughtered?"
"994","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to offer the Asr prayer when the sun was still high and bright, and a person would go to a nearby area of Madinah and reach there, and the sun was still high. This tradition has been narrated by Sayyiduna Anas with reference to some other narrators."
"995","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform the Asr prayer, then a person would go to the Quba."
"996","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: I offered Asr prayer following the example of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the sun was still bright, then I came to my family and they were sitting there. I said: Why are you people sitting?"
"997","Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) taught us the Asr prayer while the sun was still bright and shining, then I came to my family, they were sitting on the edge of Madinah, they had not prayed yet, I said: Why are you sitting?"
"998","Sayyidna Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The most distant from the Ansar was the house of Sayyidna Abul Baba, who was in Quba and the house of Abu Umays, who was in the neighborhood of Banu Haritha. These two men used to pray in the lead of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and then they reached their respective places."
"999","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates this saying of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Shall I not tell you about the prayer of the hypocrite, he keeps looking at the sun, until when the sun turns yellow and reaches between the two horns of Satan, that person gets up and bows four times, and he mentions Allah very little in this prayer."
"1000","This same hadith is narrated along with another chain of transmission on the authority of Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1001","Sayyida Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform the Asr prayer while the sun was still in my room and the shadow was not falling."
"1002","Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (RA) narrates: I performed the Asr prayer following the example of the Holy Prophet (SAW). I have a camel and I want to sacrifice it. I want you to be there, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) departed from there and we also walked. Then the camel was sacrificed and then it was prepared for us, so we ate its meat before the sun set. used to"
"1003","Sayyidna Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in the Asr prayer. When we had finished praying, a man belonging to Banusalma came to his service and said: O Messenger of Allah, we want to sacrifice a camel. That the camel was not yet sacrificed, it was sacrificed again, then its meat was prepared and cooked again, so we ate it before sunset."
"1004","Abu Qalaba says: Asr is named because it is squeezed."
"1005","Khalid al-Thuqa narrated: Hasan al-Basri, Ibn Sirin and Abu Qalaba used to perform Asr late."
"1006","Muhammad bin Hanafiyah says: Asr is named because it is squeezed."
"1007","Once Taus performed the Asr prayer late, and when he was asked about it, he said: Asr is named because it is squeezed."
"1008","Abd al-Rahman bin Yazid narrates: Sayyiduna Abdullah used to perform Asr prayer late."
"1009","Sayyidna Jabir bin Abdullah Ansari (RA) narrates: Sayyidna Gabriel (Peace be upon him) came to the service of the Prophet (PBUH) when the sun had set and said: O Muhammad (Peace be upon him): Get up and offer the Zuhr prayer. Then some time passed so that when a person's shadow became as big as his own, he came to the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the time of Asr and said: O Sayyidna Muhammad, get up and offer the Asr prayer. He performed this prayer when the sun had completely set, then some time passed until the twilight left, then Sayyiduna Gabriel (peace be upon him) came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Get up and offer the Isha prayer. The Prophet (peace be upon him) got up and he offered this prayer. Sayyidna Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) got up and prayed. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) got up and offered the morning prayer. The next day, Sayyidna Gabriel (peace be upon him) came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at noon when the shadow of the man had become as large as him, and said: O Sayyidna Muhammad (peace be upon him)! Get up and offer the Zuhr prayer. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) got up and offered the Zuhr prayer, then Sayyiduna Jibreel (peace be upon him) came to him when the shadow of a man had doubled and said: O Muhammad, get up and offer the Asr prayer. The Prophet (peace be upon him) got up and offered the Asr prayer. It was the same time (there was no delay in it). He said: Get up and offer the Maghrib prayer. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) offered the Maghrib prayer. Then Sayyiduna Gabriel (peace be upon him) came to the Prophet (peace be upon him) for the Isha prayer when a third of the night had passed and he said: Get up and offer the Isha prayer. The Prophet (peace be upon him) offered this prayer, then the Prophet He came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for the morning prayer when the light had spread well, and said: Get up and offer the morning prayer."
"1010","The same hadith is narrated from the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the authority of Sayyiduna Jabir (RA) along with another chain of transmission."
"1011","Sayyidna Jabir bin Abdullah (RA) narrates: Sayyidna Gabriel (PBUH) came to the Prophet (PBUH) to tell him about the prayer. He came to him when the sun had set. (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to you when the shadow of a man became equal to his height. Sayyidna Jibreel (peace be upon him) went ahead. The Prophet (peace be upon him) stood behind him and the people stood behind him. They stood behind him and the people stood behind the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and they offered Maghrib prayer. After that, he mentioned the rest of the hadith, but it contains these words: The next day, Sayyiduna Gabriel (peace be upon him) came to the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the sun had set, that was the same time. At the end of this tradition is this: Sayyiduna Jibreel (peace be upon him) said: There is a time (shari'a of prayers) between these two prayers. Peace be upon him said: Where is the person who inquires about prayer? Between these two (times) is the time for prayer."
"1012","Sayyidna Jabir bin Abdullah (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said this: Gabriel led me in prayer twice in Makkah, after which he mentioned the hadith, in which his words are: And performed the Maghrib prayer when the sun had set, and performed the Maghrib prayer the next day at the same time as the previous day."
"1013","Sayyiduna Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: A person came and asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the times of prayers. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) offered prayers at both times, one day at this time and one day at that time. Between these two times is (the specific time of prayers)."
"1014","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said this: Gabriel led me to prayer twice at the House of Allah, after which he mentioned the entire hadith in which he narrated the following about the second day: (The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said) He taught me the Maghrib prayer at the time when the fasting person breaks his fast. This was the same time."
"1015","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas (RA) narrates: Sayyidna Jibraeel (peace be upon him) attended the service of the Holy Prophet (PBUH) and offered all the prayers in two times, except for the Maghrib prayer (reading at the same time)."
"1016","This tradition is narrated from the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the authority of Sayyiduna Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him."
"1017","Sayyiduna Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated this: Jibreel (peace be upon him) led me in prayer twice in Makkah. The first time he came to me, then he mentioned the times of prayer (following this in the hadith are these words): The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He came to me when the sun had set."
"1018","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said this: Gabriel (peace be upon him) came to me when the morning had dawned (after that the narrator mentioned the entire hadith). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said this about Maghrib prayer: Then he came to me when the sun had set and said: Get up and offer the Maghrib prayer, so I offered three rakats of the Maghrib prayer. The narrator has mentioned this tradition as a long hadith."
"1019","Sayyidna Imam Jafar Sadiq (may Allah have mercy on him) narrates this statement of Sayyidna Jabir (may Allah have mercy on him) regarding his father (Imam Muhammad Baqir may Allah have mercy on him) that he did not delay the Maghrib prayer of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with food or any other busyness."
"1020","Imam Jafar Sadiq, may God have mercy on him, narrates this statement of his father (Imam Muhammad Baqir, may God have mercy on him). I asked Sayyidna Jabir about delaying Maghrib prayer because of dinner. Sayyidna Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not delay the Maghrib prayer because of eating or doing anything else."
"1021","Sayyiduna Abu Ayyub Ansari (RA) narrates: I heard the Prophet (PBUH) saying: Pray Maghrib before the stars rise."
"1022","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: Sayyidna Gabriel (peace be upon him) came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Makkah when the sun had set. The narrators narrated that he offered four rak'ahs of prayer in which he did not recite aloud. The people continued to follow the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and continued to pray following the example of Sayyiduna Gabriel (peace be upon him). followed the prayer of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and continued to pray following the example of Sayyiduna Gabriel (peace be upon him), then some time passed until the sun set, so he led the people in three rak'ahs, of which he recited aloud in the first two rak'ahs and did not recite aloud in the third rak'ah. He recited aloud and did not recite aloud in the last two rak'ahs, then some time passed until dawn dawned, so he led the people to pray two rak'ahs and they recited aloud in those two rak'ahs."
"1023","This tradition is transmitted as a Mursal tradition along with another Sanad."
"1024","Sayyiduna Majma bin Jariyah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the timings of the prayers, so he first offered the prayers at the beginning time, then he offered the prayers at the last time, and said: There is a time for the prayers between these two."
"1025","The sons of Sayyidna Abu Mas'ud (RA) narrate this on the authority of their father: Sayyidna Gabriel (peace be upon him) came to the service of the Prophet (PBUH) when the sun had set. Then Sayyiduna Gabriel (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the sun had set, and said: Get up and offer the prayer."
"1026","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said this: It was Gabriel (PBUH) who came to teach you about your religion, then he prayed. After that, the narrator has mentioned a tradition related to the times of the prayers, in which the words are: Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) offered the Maghrib prayer when the sun had set."
"1027","This same hadith has been narrated along with another chain of transmission, but it has these words: Then he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the next day and offered him the Maghrib prayer at the same time when the sun had set and when it becomes permissible for a fasting person to break his fast."
"1028","Muhammad bin Ammar has narrated this about Sayyidna Abu Huraira (RA), he mentioned that the Holy Prophet (PBUH) told him: Sayyidna Gabriel (PBUH) came to the Prophet (PBUH) and offered all the prayers at different times, except for Maghrib (prayer at the same time). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He came to me at Maghrib and prayed for me when the sun had set, then he came to me (i.e. the narrator says: the next day)."
"1029","Narrated by Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar: When the prayer became obligatory, Sayyidna Jibreel (peace be upon him) came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and led him the Zuhr prayer. After that, the narrator mentioned the times of the prayers, in which these words are said: Sayyidna Jibreel (peace be upon him) led the Prophet (peace be upon him) in Maghrib prayer when the sun had set. He led the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Maghrib prayer when the sun had set."
"1030","Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is a beginning time for prayer and a final time. The beginning time of Zuhr is when the sun sets and the last time is when Asr begins. The beginning time of Asr is when it begins and the last time is when the sun turns yellow. The first time of Isha is when the twilight disappears and the last time is when half of the night has passed. This tradition is not authentic as a Musnad, other narrators have quoted it as a Mursal tradition regarding Mujahid."
"1031","Mujahid narrates: It has been stated: There is a beginning time for prayer and a final time.Then he mentioned this hadith."
"1032","The same hadith is narrated by Mujahid along with another chain of transmission from the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, but it has these words: The early time of Asr is when the sun is shining until the time of Maghrib comes."
"1033","Sulaiman Bin Burida narrates this statement of his father: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him about the time of prayer. Then he commanded them, they said Iqamah for Asr while the sun was still high and bright, then the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, commanded them, so they said Iqamah for Maghrib when the sun had set, then under the command of the Prophet, they said Iqamah for the Isha prayer when the twilight had set, then under the command of the Prophet, they said Iqamah for the Fajr prayer when the morning had risen. was When the second day came, the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) instructed them to perform the Zuhr prayer at a cool time, so they performed it at a cool time, then the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon them) instructed them to perform Iqamah for Asr while the sun was still high, but the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed this prayer a little later than on the previous day. At that time, when the twilight had not yet set, then under the command of the Holy Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, he said Iqamah for Fajr, when the light had spread, then the Holy Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, inquired: Where is the person who asks about the time of prayer? So the person answered him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your prayer times are between the two that you have seen."
"1034","This tradition is narrated along with another document."
"1035","This tradition is narrated along with another document."
"1036","The same hadith is narrated along with another chain of transmission, but it contains these words: Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered them to stand for the Maghrib prayer when the sun had set, and then ordered them to stand for the Maghrib prayer the next day when the twilight had not yet set."
"1037","Abu Bakr bin Musa narrates this incident of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the authority of his father: A man came to his service and asked him about the times of the prayers. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not answer him. According to him, he said Iqamah for Zuhr prayer when the sun had set, and a person used to guess whether it was noon or not, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had more knowledge about it. According to the order of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said Iqamah for the Isha prayer at the time when the twilight had set. Then the next day, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delayed the Fajr prayer. When he finished the prayer, a person could say that the sun had risen or was about to rise. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delayed the Maghrib prayer until it was a little before sunset, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delayed the Isha prayer until a third of the night had passed."
"1038","Abu Bakr bin Abu Musa narrates the following statement from his father, Sayyiduna Abu Musa Ash’ari (RA): A man came to the Prophet (PBUH) and asked him about the prayers, but he did not give him any answer. According to him, he said Iqamah for Zuhr prayer at the time when a person thinks that the sun has set or has not yet set, by the way, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has more knowledge about it. Iqamah was said for Isha prayer at the time when the twilight had set. The narrator narrates: The next day, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) offered the Fajr prayer at a time when people thought that the sun had risen or had not yet risen. Well, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) knew more about this. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) offered the Zuhr prayer at the time when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) offered the Asr prayer on the first day. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) offered the Maghrib prayer shortly before the sunset, then he offered the Isha prayer after a third of the night had passed, then he inquired, \Where is the questioner?\"" Between these two times is the time (of prayers)."""
"1039","The same hadith along with another chain of transmission has been narrated from the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the authority of Abu Bakr bin Musa, on the authority of his father. \So he said Iqamah for the Maghrib prayer when the sun had set"
"1040","Abdullah bin Burida narrates the statement of his father that the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In addition to the Maghrib prayer, two rak'ahs (Nafl Yasunat) will be performed between Harazan and Iqamah."
"1041","Hayan bin Ubaidullah Adwi narrates: Once we were sitting with Hazrat Abdullah bin Burida, may Allah be pleased with him, and the muezzin gave the adhan for the Zuhr prayer. The Rak'ats will be performed. Abdullah Bin Burida says: I saw Sayyiduna Abdullah Bin Umar performing these two Rak'ats even at Maghrib. He would not abandon them under any circumstances. The narrator narrates: We got up and we prayed two rak'ahs before Iqamah, then we waited until the Imam came, then we performed the obligatory prayer following him. Husain Mualim, Saeed Jariri, Kahams bin Hasan, have narrated traditions contrary to him, all these narrators are reliable, while the narrator named Hayan bin Ubaidullah is not very authentic; the rest is known to Allah."
"1042","Abdullah bin Burida narrates this statement of Sayyiduna Abdullah Muzni. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said this: perform two rak'ahs of Nawafil before the Maghrib duties. (Imam Darqutni, may God have mercy on him) says: This hadith is more authentic than the previous one."
"1043","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Mughfil (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said, “The Nafl prayer will be offered between the Azan and the Iqamah,” (he repeated this sentence twice) and then he said, “This order is for him who does not want to gamble.”"
"1044","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Mughfil (RA) narrates that the Prophet (PBUH) said that whoever wants to perform the Nafl prayer between the Adhan and Iqamah."
"1045","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Burida (RA) narrates this statement of his father: The Prophet (PBUH) said that the Nafl prayer should be performed between the Azan and the Iqamah."
"1046","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Zubair (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said that two rak'ahs (Sunnah Yanfal) should be performed before every obligatory prayer. These words are from a narrator named Ibn Dawud: A narrator named Ismail has quoted the word, mafuruzat, instead of the word, maktuba."
"1047","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: Sometimes a stranger would enter the mosque of Madinah and people were praying there at Maghrib, so it was felt that the people had already prayed (in congregation), because there were so many people who prayed two rak'ahs before the Maghrib prayer."
"1048","Abd al-Aziz Lebanese narrates: I heard Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik narrating that it was the custom of the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, that when the muezzin gave the Maghrib call to prayer, they would rush to the pillars to pray two rak'ahs before Maghrib."
"1049","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: When the Companions heard the Maghrib call to prayer, they would get up and perform Nawafil as if it were obligatory."
"1050","Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: During the time of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), we used to perform Dunaafal before Maghrib. The narrator narrates: We asked Sayyiduna Anas: Did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) see you people performing this prayer? So Sayyiduna Anas said: The Prophet has seen us perform this prayer, but he did not order us to perform it, nor did he ever forbid it."
"1051","Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (may God be pleased with him) narrates: It was our custom in Madinah when the Maghrib call to prayer was given, we people would quickly move towards the pillars and pray two rak'ahs (as Nawafil before the obligatory prayer) until a stranger would enter the mosque and he would feel that perhaps the prayer had been offered, because there were a large number of those who offered these two rak'ahs."
"1052","Shaykh Abul Khair narrates: Shaykh Abu Tamim Jaishani got up and started offering two rak'ahs before Maghrib prayer, so I said to Sayyiduna Uqbah bin Amir: Pay attention to them: Which prayer are they praying? Sayyiduna Uqbah turned to him and said: This is the prayer we used to perform during the time of the Holy Prophet (peace be upon him)."
"1053","Muhammad bin Abd al-Rahman narrates: The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There are two types of righteous morning, one is the righteous morning which spreads (in the width direction) like the tail of a snake. is) and forbids eating for the person who breaks the fast (i.e., the time for the fast ends at that time)."
"1054","Sayyidna Ubada bin Samit (RA) and Sayyidna Shaddad Bin Aws (RA) said: There are two types of twilight, red and white. When the reddish twilight sets, it is time for prayer, and in the same way, morning dawn is of two types, one with length and one with width. When the width ends, then the time for prayer begins."
"1055","Sayyidna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) says: Shafaq is called red."
"1056","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said this, \Shafaq\"" means red"
"1057","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: Shafaq refers to redness."
"1058","Sayyiduna Numan bin Basheer (RA) narrates: I have the most knowledge about the Isha prayer at this time, the Prophet (PBUH) used to perform it when the moon sets on the third night."
"1059","The same hadith has been narrated about the Holy Prophet, peace and blessings of God be upon him, along with another chain of transmission, but it has these words: (The narrator is in doubt) The moon of the third night or the moon of the fourth night (used to set). Some other narrators have also narrated it on the authority of Sayyiduna Nu'man (RA), and they have quoted in it the words, the moon of the third night:"
"1060","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said this (according to Madinah Munawarah): The place between the east and the west is the Qiblah."
"1061","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said this (according to Madinah Munawarah): The place between the east and the west is the Qiblah."
"1062","Sayyidna Jabir bin Abdullah (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) sent out an expedition. I was also present in it. Once, in extreme darkness, we could not figure out the direction of the Qibla. So many of us said: \We have guessed the Qibla"
"1063","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) says that this verse was specifically revealed about the Nafl prayer, that is, whichever way your riding is, you can pray facing that way."
"1064","Sayyiduna Jabir bin Abdullah (RA) narrates: We were on a journey with the Prophet (PBUH) and during that time it became dark, we were very worried and there was a disagreement between us about the Qiblah. Each of us prayed facing our favorite direction and some people also marked a mark in front of us so that we would remember our place. Diya, you said this, your prayer has been correct. It is narrated in the same way on the authority of a narrator named Muhammad bin Salim, while some other narrators have narrated it from another chain of transmission and both of these narrators are weak."
"1065","Abdullah bin Aamir narrates this statement of his father: Once during the time of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) we prayed on a very dark night during a journey. We did not know which direction the Qibla was facing. \Wherever you are"
"1066","This tradition is narrated along with another chain of transmission, but it contains these words: Each of us prayed facing the stones in front of us. When morning came, our direction was not towards the Qiblah."
"1067","This tradition is narrated along with another document."
"1068","Sayyiduna Malik bin Hawairat (RA) narrates: We came to the service of the Prophet (PBUH), we were young people, our ages were close to each other, and we stayed in his service for twenty days. People, now go back to your homes, and stay there, educate these people, treat them well, just as you saw me praying, continue to pray in the same way, when it is time for prayer, one of you should give the call to prayer, and the one who is older among you should lead you."
"1069","This tradition is narrated from the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the authority of Sayyidna Malik bin Haweerith along with another chain of transmission, but it contains these words: \You people should pray in the same way as you have seen me praying.\"""""
"1070","Sayyiduna Abu Saeed Al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates the saying of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that when three men are together, one of them should lead them, (i.e. they should perform the prayer in congregation) and the one who can recite the Qur’aat well (or memorizes the most verses) will be more entitled to lead the prayer."
"1071","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: Once some people were performing the morning prayer in the Kaaba, while a person came to them and said: Last night, the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) received the order of the Qur’an. You should also turn towards it (the narrator says) At that time, these people were facing towards Syria (i.e. Bayt al-Maqdis), so they turned around and started facing the Kaaba (praying) at that time."
"1072","Sayyiduna Bara' (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: After the arrival of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Madinah, for about sixteen months, we people followed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by turning towards Bait-ul-Maqdis and praying. \We see your face rising towards the sky"
"1073","Sayyidna Anas bin Malik (RA) narrates: A person announced on behalf of the Holy Prophet (SAW) that the Kaaba has been made the Qiblah (the narrator says)."
"1074","Sayyiduna Sahl bin Sa'idi (may God be pleased with him) narrates: When the Kaaba was made the Qiblah, a person passed by the people living in the Kaabah, who were praying at that time."
"1075","Sayyidna Jabir bin Abdullah (RA) narrates: Sayyidna Mu'adh (RA) used to perform the Isha prayer following the example of the Prophet (PBUH) and then he would go back to his neighborhood and lead those people in prayer."
"1076","Sayyidna Jabir bin Abdullah (RA) narrates: Sayyidna Mu'adh (RA) used to perform the Isha prayer following the example of the Prophet (PBUH), and then he would go back to his neighborhood and lead those people in prayer."
"1077","Abd al-Malik bin Rabi', on the authority of his father, narrates the statement of his grandfather: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the prayer in the pen of camels, but he permitted the prayer in the pen of the goats. A narrator named Ibn Sa'id has said these words: The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has commanded us: We should pray in the sheepfolds, and you have forbidden us in this regard, we should pray in the camel folds."
"1078","Abd al-Malik bin Rabi', on the authority of his father, narrates the statement of his grandfather: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pray in the sheep pen, do not pray in the camel pen."
"1079","Abd al-Malik Ibn Rabi' narrated his grandfather's statement on the authority of his father: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade praying in the pen of camels."
"1080","Sayyidina Yazid bin Aamir (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said to me: When you (come to the mosque) for prayer and find people praying, pray with them."
"1081","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: A person came. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had finished praying at that time. He got up and began to pray alone. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who will cooperate with him? He should perform this prayer with him (again)."
"1082","Sayyiduna Asma bin Malik (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) was sitting in the mosque after performing the Zuhr prayer, while a person came in and began to pray. The Prophet (PBUH) said, \Would anyone get up and do good to him so that he could pray with him?\"""""
"1083","Sayyidna Samura bin Jundab (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said: When there are two people, they should stand together and pray, and if there are three, then one of them should go ahead."
"1084","Sayyidah Umm Waraqah (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave them this permission, that the Adhan should be given for them and Iqamah should be called for them, and that they should lead the women (i.e. lead them in congregational prayer)."
"1085","Sayyiduna Uqbah bin Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Imam of the people should be the one who migrated first. Don't lead him and don't sit in his designated place, but he can sit with his permission. (The narrator narrates): At the beginning of the prayer, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to straighten our shoulders."
"1086","Sayyiduna Abu Mas'ud Radiyallahu Anhu narrates: The Prophet, may God's prayers and peace be upon him, said: The person who knows the Qur'an more should lead the people."
"1087","Sayyiduna Abu Musa Ash'ari (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said: Two or more people are a congregation."
"1088","Amr ibn Shuaib narrates this statement of his grandfather regarding his father: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two men and more people are a congregation (i.e. they should pray in congregation)."
"1089","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said: No villager, no foreigner, and no minor boy should stand in the first row."
"1090","Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) says: The rules of the Sunnah include that when the Imam returns the salam, he should not stand and offer Nawafl at the same place where a person has performed the obligatory prayer, but rather move away from there to one side or perform Nawafl by talking in between."
"1091","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: A person stood up and asked, O Messenger of Allah: Can a person pray in one piece of cloth? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, \All of you people have two clothes?\"" The narrator narrates: During the time of Sayyiduna Umar"
"1092","Urwa bin Mughirah narrated this statement of his father: The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No Prophet died until a person from his people led him. (Imam Darqutni, may God have mercy on him, says this) A narrator of this hadith, Ibn Abu Umayyah, is not authentic."
"1093","Sayyidna Nu'man bin Bashir (RA) narrates: Once the Prophet (PBUH) turned to the people and said: Straighten your ranks. The Prophet (PBUH) said this three times. (Says the narrator) So I saw every person next to him shin with his shin, his knee with his shoulder, his shoulder with his shoulder (i.e. trying to make the row perfectly straight)."
"1094","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Masoud (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) used to hold his left hand with his right hand during prayer."
"1095","Sayyida Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates: There are three things (in the routines of Prophethood): breaking the fast early, making the Suhoor late, and placing the right hand over the left during prayer."
"1096","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said this: We (i.e.) the Prophets have been commanded: We should break the fast early, make the suhoor late, and place our right hands over our left hands during prayer."
"1097","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas (RA) narrates the saying of the Prophet (PBUH): We Prophets have been commanded to delay Suhoor and break fast and keep our right hands over our left hands during prayer."
"1098","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) says that it is Sunnah to place one hand over the other during prayer."
"1099","Sayyidna Ali (RA) says: (Allah is the guide) So pray for your Lord and sacrifice."
"1100","Qabaisa bin Halab narrates this statement of his father: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placing his right hand on his left hand during prayer."
"1101","Alqamah bin Wail Hazrami narrates the statement of his father: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placing his right hand on his left hand during prayer."
"1102","Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) says: It is Sunnah to place one hand on the other hand below the navel during prayer."
"1103","Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) says: Among the sunnahs of prayer, this is also included, placing the right hand on the left hand below the navel."
"1104","Alqamah bin Wail narrated this statement from his father: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he stood up to pray, and he held (the wrist) of his left hand with his right hand."
"1105","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Masoud (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw me. I was placing my left hand on my right hand during prayer, so the Prophet (peace be upon him) took my right hand and placed it on my left."
"1106","Sayyiduna Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a gentleman who had his left hand on his right hand (after this is according to the previous hadith)."
"1107","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Masoud (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: Once the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him. He was praying at that time. He was placing his left hand on his right hand."
"1108","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would stand for prayer, he would say this and that about the right and the left, and then he would say: Keep (the row) straight and be equal."
"1109","Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood up for prayer, he would say Takbeer and raise both hands up to his shoulders. When he finished his recitation and started bowing, he would do the same. When he rose from Bowing, he would do the same again. After that, he would not bow during the prayer. When he was sitting, when he would stand up after performing two Rak'ahs, then he would bow and say Takbeer in the same way. would say"
"1110","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: When the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood for prayer, he would raise both his hands until they were at the level of his shoulders, then he would say Takbeer, then when he started to bow down, he would do it, then when he raised his head from bowing, he would do it."
"1111","Salim narrates the statement of his father (Sayyidna Abdullah bin Umar, may Allah be pleased with him): When the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, would stand for prayer, he would raise both of his hands until they both came against his shoulders, then the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, would say Takbeer with him, then when he started to bow, he would raise them both until they both came against his shoulders, then the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, would go into bowing. When he got up, he would raise both of them until they were facing his shoulders, then he would recite, Sama'Allah Liman Hamda, then the Prophet (PBUH) would go into prostration."
"1112","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (RA) narrates: I saw the Prophet (PBUH) stand up to pray, then he raised both his hands until they both came to his shoulders, then he said Takbeer, and he did the same when he raised his blessed head from Ruku, then he recited, ``Sama'Allah Liman Hamda'', but when he raised his head from Sajdah, the Prophet (PBUH) did not do so at that time."
"1113","The same hadith regarding Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (RA) is present along with another chain of transmission, it has these words: He performed Rifa Yaddin and then said Takbeer."
"1114","Sayyiduna Abdullah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: When the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood up to pray, he would raise both his hands until they reached the level of the Prophet’s (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shoulders and he would say Takbeer (after that, according to the previous hadith)."
"1115","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to raise both his hands until they were equal to or close to his shoulders (after that he narrated according to the previous hadith)."
"1116","The same hadith is narrated from Zahri along with another chain of transmission, in which there are these words: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said Takbeer at the beginning of the prayer, he would raise both hands while he was saying Takbeer, until those two hands came to the level of his shoulders (after that, according to the previous hadith)."
"1117","Salim narrates: Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated this: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would stand for prayer, he would raise both his hands up to his shoulders and say Takbeer, then when he started to bow, he would raise his hands to his shoulders and say Takbeer. Laiya who narrated his praise) then you would go into prostration, you would not perform Rifa Yadeen between Prostrations, you would perform Rifa Yadeen while saying Takbeer in every rak'at when you would say Takbeer before going into Ruku until your prayer was complete (ie you used to do this during the whole prayer)."
"1118","Narrated by Nafi': When Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (RA) saw a person who bowed down while praying and did not make rifa'idin, he used to pick him up to hit him with pebbles until that person started making rifa'idin."
"1119","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to bow down while bowing at the beginning of the prayer. This hadith has been narrated only by Abd al-Wahhab as an authentic hadith on the authority of Hameed."
"1120","Sayyiduna Wail bin Hajar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the beginning of the prayer, he bowed his hands until both of his hands were at the level of his shoulders, then when he started to bow (he did so), then when he raised his head (he did it at that time), he (while sitting) placed his right hand on his right knee and his left hand on his left knee. (of the right hand) made a circle and pointed thus, the narrator named Sufyan said by pointing with his index finger. The narrator narrates: I attended the service of these gentlemen (i.e. the Companions) and I found them in such a state that they used to pray in the cold season even with a cloak over them."
"1121","Hussain bin Abd al-Rahman narrates: We attended the service of Ibrahim Nakhai, and Amr bin Marwah told him this. So Ibrahim Nakhai said: I understand that your father must have seen the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) doing this for only one day, and he would have remembered this, so did Sayyidna Abdullah (bin Mas'ud) not remember this? After that, Ibrahim Nakhai said: Rifa Yaddin will be done only at the beginning of the prayer."
"1122","Sayyiduna Wail bin Hajar (RA) narrates: I saw the Prophet (PBUH) going into Ruku at the beginning of the prayer, and when he recited Samaa Allah Liman Hamda (i.e. getting up from Ruku) he raised both his hands to his ears (i.e. raised his hands)."
"1123","Sayyidna Malik bin Hawairat (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform Rifa Yadeen at the beginning of the prayer, while going into Ruku, and when he raised his head from Ruku. A narrator named Ibn Mubashir narrated these words: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to bow at the beginning of the prayer, then when he started bowing, when he raised his head from bowing (even then he bowed). A narrator named Abu Awana has narrated this: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said the Takbeer, he raised his hands up to his shoulders, when he went into the bow, when he raised his head from the bow, and when he recited, Sama'Allah Liman Hamda."
"1124","Sayyiduna Abu Musa Ash'ari (RA) said, \Shall I show you the way of prayer of the Prophet (PBUH)?\"" Then he said Takbeer and raised both hands"
"1125","This same hadith has been narrated in another chain of transmission from the Holy Prophet (peace be upon him). The Al-Durawis have narrated it as a marfu' hadith on the authority of Hamad, while the rest of the narrators besides these two have narrated it as a muqaf tradition. I have heard Qazi Abu Ja'far saying: At first I had the same position as the people of Iraq, but then I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying in a dream."
"1126","Sayyidna Bara bin Azib (RA) narrates: When the Prophet (PBUH) used to raise both his hands while saying Takbeer, we used to see that both thumbs of the Prophet (PBUH) came close to his ears."
"1127","Abd al-Rahman bin Abu Laili narrates: I heard Sayyiduna Bara' (RA) narrating this hadith in that gathering, in which Sayyidna Ka'b bin Ajrah (RA) was present."
"1128","Abdullah bin Mubarak says: According to me, this tradition narrated by Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Masoud, may God be pleased with him, is not authentic. According to me, the hadith in which it is mentioned is authentic: The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed Rif Yaddin while going into Ruku and raising his head from Ruku. Abdullah bin Mubarak has narrated this: Ubaidullah Umari, Imam Malik, Muammar, Sufyan, Yunus, Muhammad bin Abu Hafsa have narrated this tradition from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the authority of Zuhri, on the authority of Salem on the authority of his father (Sayyidna Abdullah bin Umar)."
"1129","Sayyiduna Bara' (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: He saw the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) making ablution at the beginning of the prayer."
"1130",": This tradition is narrated from Sayyiduna bin Azib, may Allah be pleased with him, along with another chain of transmission."
"1131","Sayyiduna Bara' (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: He saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he stood up for prayer, and he said Takbeer and said, ``Refu Yadin.'' A narrator named Yazid was exhorted in his last years to recite these words: Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not do so, so he accepted this exhortation, the words of this tradition were confused for him."
"1132","Sayyiduna Bara bin Azib (RA) narrates: I saw the Holy Prophet (SAW) when he stood up to pray, he (SAW) raised both hands up to his ears while saying Takbeer. Narrated Ali bin Asim: When I came to Kufa, I was told that a narrator named Yazid was alive. I went to his service and he narrated this hadith to me. I said to him: I have been told this tradition by Ibn Abi Layli, who has also quoted these words: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not make ablution later, so he replied: I do not remember these words."
"1133","Sayyidna Abdullah bin Masoud (RA) narrates: I have performed prayers following the example of the Prophet (PBUH), Sayyidna Abu Bakr (RA) and Sayyidna Umar (RA). A narrator named Ishaq has stated this: We will adopt this tradition about the way of praying. The narrator Muhammad bin Jabir is unique in narrating this tradition and this Sahib is weak. Muhammad bin Jabir narrated it from Ibrahim Nakhai with reference to a narrator named Hamad, while other narrators besides Hamad narrated it from Ibrahim Nakhai as a messenger tradition from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Masoud Radiyallahu Anhu as his own action. But he did not copy, which is related to the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and this tradition is correct."
"1134","Sayyiduna Wail bin Hajar (RA) narrates: I attended the service of the Holy Prophet (SAW) to see for myself how the Prophet (SAW) prayed. When he raised his head, he raised both hands as much. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went into prostration, he placed his two hands against the same part of his head."
"1135","This tradition is narrated from Wail bin Hajar along with another chain of transmission, but it does not mention prostration."
"1136","This same hadith has been narrated along with another chain of transmission on the authority of Sayyidna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him). Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: At the beginning of the prayer, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would raise both hands up to his shoulders, when he (may Allah’s prayers and peace be upon him) went into ruku’, and when he raised his head from ruku’, he would raise his hands in the same way."
"1137","Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: When the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to start the prayer, after saying the Takbeer, he would recite this dua: \I have turned my face to the One who created the heavens and the earth properly"
"1138","Sayyiduna Ali bin Abu Talib (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) used to recite the following at the beginning of the obligatory prayer: \I have turned my face to the One who created the heavens and the earth. I have turned (toward Allah) as a Muslim"
"1139","Sayyidna Jabir bin Abdullah (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) used to recite the following when he started the prayer: \Indeed"
"1140","Sayyidna Abu Saeed Al-Khudri (RA) narrates: When the Prophet (PBUH) used to stand up at night to offer Nawafil, after saying Takbeer at the beginning of the prayer, he used to recite: \So pure"
"1141","Sayyida Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite this at the beginning of the prayer: \So pure"
"1142","Sayyidna Abdullah bin Umar (RA) narrates this statement of Sayyidna Umar Bin Khattab: When the Holy Prophet (PBUH) used to say Takbeer for prayer, he would recite: \So pure"
"1143","Narrated Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar: When Sayyiduna Umar said Takbeer for prayer, he used to recite this dua: You are pure, O Allah! Praise is reserved for You, Your name is blessed, Your majesty is high and superior, and there is no god but You."
"1144","Aswad narrates this about Sayyiduna Umar: When he started the prayer, he used to recite: Glory be to You, O Allah, praise be to You, Your name is blessed, Your majesty is exalted and exalted, and there is no god but You."
"1145","Alqamah narrates, he attended the service of Sayyidna Umar ibn al-Khattab and when he saw Sayyidna Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, start the prayer, he recited: You are holy, O Allah."
"1146","Al-Aswad narrates: I saw Sayyiduna Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, when he started the prayer, he recited this after saying Takbeer."
"1147","The same tradition is narrated along with another document, however, these words are added to the names, after that they used to read ta'awz."
"1148","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: At the beginning of the prayer, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to raise both his hands until his thumbs reached the level of his ears after saying the Takbeer."
"1149","Sayyida Ayesha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite this at the beginning of the prayer: So pure O Allah! Praise be to You, blessed is Your name, exalted is Your majesty, there is no god but You."
"1150","This tradition is narrated along with another document, in which these words are added: \You used to raise both your hands up to your shoulders and then read: To Paq Hai O Allah!\"""""
"1151","Al-Qamah and Aswad narrate: Sayyiduna Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, used to recite the Takbeer. \So pure"
"1152","Ata narrates: Ubaid bin Umayr and I attended the service of Sayyida Aisha Siddiqa. We asked her about the beginning of the Prophet's prayer, so she said: When the Prophet said the Takbeer, he used to recite: \Glory be to Allah"
"1153","Aswad narrates: Sayyiduna Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, used to recite: \Glory be to Allah"
"1154","Abu Wail narrates: Syedna Uthman Ghani used to recite this dua at the beginning of the prayer: \So pure"
"1155","Sayyidna Imam Hasan (RA) narrates this statement of Sayyidna Ali Ibn Abu Talib (RA) that the Holy Prophet (PBUH) used to recite the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Merciful in prayer."
"1156","Sayyiduna Ali bin Abu Talib (RA) narrates: The Holy Prophet (SAW) used to recite Bismillah-e-Rahman-e-Raheem in a loud voice along with both surahs."
"1157","Imam Abu Ja'far Sadiq (may Allah have mercy on him) narrates this statement of Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding his father (Imam Baqir) regarding his father Imam Zain al-Abidin (may Allah have mercy on him) regarding his father Imam Hussain, he says: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you stand up for prayer, how do you show respect? I said, I am reciting Alhamdulillah, Lord of the worlds."
"1158","Sayyidna Ali (RA) and Sayyidna Ammar (RA) say that the Holy Prophet (PBUH) used to recite Bismillah Al-Rahman al-Rahim in a loud voice during obligatory prayers."
"1159","Both Sayyidna Ali (RA) and Sayyidna Ammar (RA) narrated that the Holy Prophet (PBUH) used to recite Bismillah Al-Rahman Al-Rahim in a loud voice."
"1160","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas (RA) narrates that the Prophet (PBUH) used to recite Bismillah-e-Rahman-e-Raheem in a loud voice in prayer."
"1161","Ahmad bin Muhammad narrates this statement of his grandfather regarding his father, Amirul Momineen Mahdi led us to Maghrib prayer, he recited Bismillah Rahman Al Rahim in a loud voice. The narrator says: I said: Amirul Momineen, what is this way? So he said, \My father has narrated this statement of Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas"
"1162","Sayyiduna Ibn Abbas narrates that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim in a loud voice at the beginning of the prayer."
"1163","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas (RA) narrates that the Holy Prophet (SAW) used to recite Bismillah, Rahman, Raheem, together with the two Surahs in a loud voice until he passed away."
"1164","It has been narrated about Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) that he used to recite Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim in a loud voice and it was mentioned that the Prophet also used to recite it loudly."
"1165","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) used to recite Bismillah Rahman Raheem at the beginning of prayer. A narrator named Nishapuri used to recite these words."
"1166","Sayyiduna Umar (RA) narrates that I performed prayers following the example of Sayyidna Abu Bakr (RA) and Sayyidna Umar (RA)."
"1167","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said, \When Gabriel came to me with the revelation"
"1168","Naeem bin Abdullah narrates: I prayed following the example of Sayyiduna Abu Huraira, may Allah be pleased with him. He recited Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim, then he recited Surah Al-Fatiha until he reached this verse, Ghir al-Maghdhub alihim wala al-Dhaaleen, then he said Amen. He said: \By the One in Whose Hand is my life"
"1169","This tradition is also narrated along with some other isnads."
"1170","This tradition is also narrated along with some other isnads."
"1171","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: When the Holy Prophet (SAW) used to recite recitations while teaching the people to pray, he used to start the recitation with the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Merciful. Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrated: This is a verse from the Book of Allah. If you wish, you can recite it at the beginning of the Qur’an. A narrator named Farsi narrated these words: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to lead the people, he used to recite Bismillah, Rahman al-Rahim, and he did not quote any other words."
"1172","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said this: Jibraeel told me the way of prayer, he got up, he said Takbeer in front of us, then he recited Bismillah, Rahman, Raheem, in this prayer, which is recited aloud, and he did not do this in every rak'ah."
"1173","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said this."
"1174","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) used to recite Bismillah, Rahman, Raheem in a loud voice. The correct one is Abu Mushar."
"1175","Sayyidah Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates: The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in the same way: Bismillah, Rahman, Raheem, Al-Hamdulillah, Rabb, Al-Amin, Rahman, Raheem, Ta Wala, Al-Dhaaleen. He used to recite these verses one by one."
"1176","Imam Jafar Sadiq (may Allah have mercy on him) narrates this statement of Sayyiduna Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding his father (Imam Baqir), the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked how you recite when you stand for prayer."
"1177","Qatadah narrates: Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (RA) was asked how the Prophet (PBUH) used to recite Qaraat. He replied, “It was by stretching the Qaraat."
"1178","Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (RA) narrates: I heard the Prophet (PBUH) reciting Bismillah, Rahman, Raheem in a loud voice."
"1179","Muhammad bin Mutawakkil narrates: I have performed Fajr and Maghrib prayers so many times following the example of Mu'tamar bin Sulaiman: I cannot count them. I used to read Bismillah Rahman Raheem. I heard Mu'tamar say: I will always follow my father's way of praying, my father has narrated this."
"1180","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite Bismillah, Rahman, Raheem in a loud voice."
"1181","Sayyidna Nu'man bin Bashir (RA) narrates that the Prophet (PBUH) said this: \Sayyidna Jibraeel led me in prayer near the Ka'ba"
"1182","Sayyiduna Samrah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite the sukta twice (while reciting) one sukta when he recited Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim and one sukta when he finished reciting. Sayyidna Imran bin Hussain (RA) did not accept this, so people wrote a letter to Sayyidna Abi Bin Ka'b (RA) about this, and he wrote in reply: Samra has stated correctly."
"1183","Ibn Burida narrates this statement of his father: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, “Before leaving the mosque, I will teach you a verse (maybe the narrator doubts this word), a surah, which was revealed to me alone among the prophets who came after Sulaiman (peace be upon him). The narrator narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) got up and walked, so I also followed him. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached the door of the mosque, he took one foot out from the door of the mosque and the other foot was still inside the mosque. What do you start with? The narrator says: I said: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is the same, this is the same, then you visited."
"1184","Abdullah bin Buraidah narrates this statement of his father Sayyiduna Buraidah (RA) I heard the Prophet (PBUH) reciting Bismillah Al-Rahman al-Rahim in a loud voice. Narrated by Abdullah bin Burida: Sayyidna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) used to recite it aloud, likewise Sayyidna Abdullah bin Abbas and Sayyidna Muhammad bin Hanafyah (may Allah be pleased with him) also used to recite it aloud."
"1185","Sayyiduna Haqma bin Umayr (may Allah be pleased with him) who is a Badri (companion) narrates that I prayed in the footsteps of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1186","Sayyida Ayesha Siddiqa narrates that the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim in a loud voice."
"1187","Sayyidna Anas bin Malik (RA) narrated: Sayyidna Mu'awiya (RA) prayed in Madinah, he recited it aloud, but he did not recite Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim aloud, nor did he recite Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim before Surah Fatiha, nor did he recite Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim before the last Surah. His voice could reach the Muhajirin and the Ansar, who prayed and greeted them, and addressed them in a loud voice and said: O Sayyidna Muawiya, have you shortened the prayer or have you forgotten? The narrator narrates that after that he recited Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim in a loud voice in every prayer he performed, before Surah Al-Fatiha and before the Surah after Al-Fatiha (recited aloud Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim) and also recited Takbeer while prostrating. All the narrators of this tradition are authentic."
"1188","Ismail bin Ubaid narrates this statement of his grandfather regarding his father: Sayyidna Muawiya bin Abu Sufyan (RA) came to Madinah to perform Hajj or Umrah. He did not recite Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim (in a loud voice) at the beginning of the recitation while leading the people and started reciting Surah Fatiha. You have intentionally omitted or forgot to recite Bismillah Rahman Raheem. (The narrator narrates: When Sayyiduna Muawiya (RA) led these people in the next prayer, he recited Bismillah, Rahman, Raheem in a loud voice). Imam Dar Qutni, may God have mercy on him, narrates: In addition to the traditions that we have quoted about the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, reciting Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim out loud, some other companions and pure wives have also narrated this. But those traditions have also been quoted which make it clear which of the Companions of the Holy Prophet and the scholars who came after them used to recite Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim in a loud voice."
"1189","Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates the saying of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who does not recite Surah Al-Fatiha while praying, his prayer is not complete. The narrator narrates: I said: O Sayyidna Abu Huraira! Sometimes I follow the example of the Imam, he pinched (something) on my shin and said: Then recite it in your heart, because I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Allah Ta'ala says: I have divided the prayer (reciting) into two parts between myself and my servants, and half of it is for him. My servant mentioned my greatness then he says, Ayak Nabad Wayak Nasta'in, so this verse will be divided into two parts between me and my servant, the last part of the surah is for my servant and what my servant asked for he will get. The narrator named Ibn Simaan is Abdullah bin Simaan and this person is a abandoned hadeeth. A group of reliable narrators have narrated this hadith on the authority of Alaa bin Abd al-Rahman. These narrators include Imam Malik bin Anas, Shaykh Ibn Jarij, Shaykh Ruh bin Qasim, Shaykh Ibn Aayynah, Ibn Ajlan, Hasan bin Hur, Abu Wais and others. These men have quoted differences in the authenticity of this hadith, but they agree on the text. So it will be considered correct to quote all these gentlemen unanimously against the narration narrated by Ibn Samaan, Allah knows best."
"1190","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said this: When you recite Alhamdulillah, Lord of the Worlds, recite Bismillah, Rahman, Rahim, because it is Umm Al-Qur’an, Umm Al-Katab, and Sabaa Mastani, and Bismillah, Rahman Al-Rahim is one of its verses."
"1191","Sayyida Umm Salma (RA) narrates that when the Holy Prophet (PBUH) used to recite Qur’aat, he used to recite each verse separately. Abdullah ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Umar ibn Harun narrated it on the authority of Ibn Jarij and added some additional words."
"1192","Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite Alhamdulillah, Lord of the worlds, at the beginning of the prayer, and then he would remain silent for a while. This hadith has been narrated by the narrator named Abu Dawood only as a superior hadith with respect to Shu'ba, while other narrators have narrated it as a muwaqf tradition as the work of Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA)."
"1193","Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (RA) narrates: I saw the Prophet (PBUH) praying wearing shoes and wearing socks."
"1194","Abd Khair narrates that Sayyiduna Ali was asked about the seven bladders, and he said, Alhamdulillah. He said to him: Yes, these are six verses."
"1195","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abu Awfi (RA) narrates: A man came to the service of the Holy Prophet (SAW) and mentioned that he could not memorize the Qur'an. A narrator named Ibn Uyaynah narrated these words: He said, O Messenger of Allah, teach me something that will replace the Qur'an for me, because I cannot recite it. \Glory be to Allah. All praise is reserved for Allah and there is no god but Allah. Allah is the Greatest and nothing can happen without Allah's help.\"" The narrator narrates: He closed his fingers (remembered these words) and then said: This is for the praise of my Lord. What will happen to me (as a supplication)? Then the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said"
"1196","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abu Awfi (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: A person came to the service of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he said, \O Messenger of Allah"
"1197","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abu Awfi (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: A person came to the service of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said, “O Messenger of Allah, I cannot learn the Qur’an. \In the name of Allah (I begin my work receiving blessings) All praises are reserved for Allah. There is no god but Allah. Allah is the Greatest.\"""""
"1198","Ibn Abi Malikah narrates: I heard Sayyidah Sayyidah Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, when she was asked about a verse of the Qur'an, and she recited: I begin by seeking blessings in the name of Allah, the Most Merciful and the Most Merciful. \Those who follow what is similar to it in order to seek temptation and seek its interpretation"
"1199","Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (RA) narrates: I prayed following the Prophet (PBUH), Sayyidna Abu Bakr, Sayyidna Umar, and Sayyidna Uthman RA."
"1200","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: I prayed following the example of the Prophet (peace be upon him), Sayyidna Abu Bakr, Sayyidna Umar and Sayyidna Uthman (may God be pleased with him). The same tradition is also reported in some other isnads, but there are some word differences in it."
"1201","Sayyidna Anas (RA) narrates: I prayed following the example of the Prophet (PBUH), Sayyidna Abu Bakr, Sayyidna Umar, and Sayyidna Uthman (RA)."
"1202","This tradition has also been narrated along with some other isnads, but there is some wording difference in it. This tradition has also been followed."
"1203","Sayyidna Anas bin Malik (RA) narrates: I prayed following the example of the Prophet (PBUH), Sayyidna Abu Bakr, Sayyidna Umar, and Sayyidna Uthman (RA)."
"1204","Sayyidna Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) Sayyidna Abu Bakr and Sayyidna Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) did not recite Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim in a loud voice. Narrated by Allah Anhu."
"1205","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to begin the recitation with Sayyidna Abu Bakr, Sayyidna Umar and Sayyidna Uthman Ghani (may Allah be pleased with them) (in a loud voice during the prayer) with Alhamdulillah, Lord of the worlds."
"1206","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: I have prayed following the example of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Sayyidna Abu Bakr, Sayyidna Umar and Sayyidna Uthman Ghani (may Allah be pleased with them)."
"1207","Sayyidna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: We have prayed following the example of the Prophet (peace be upon him), Sayyidna Abu Bakr, Sayyidna Umar and Sayyidna Usman Ghani."
"1208","Saeed bin Yazid Azdi narrates: I asked Sayyidna Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to start the recitation (in a loud voice) with Alhamdulillah, Lord of the Worlds or Bismillah, Rahman al-Raheem. (The narrator says) I inquired: Did the Prophet pray wearing shoes."
"1209","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said that whoever prays with the Imam should recite Surah Al-Fatihah during the Imam's sukkah, and whoever recites the entire Surah, then it is sufficient for him to do so. A narrator named Muhammad bin Abdullah bin Ubayd is weak."
"1210","Yazid bin Sharik narrates: He asked Sayyiduna Umar (RA) about the issue of reciting in the imitation of the Imam, so he said, \You should recite Surah Fatiha.\"" (The narrator says) I inquired: Although (Imam) you? So he said"
"1211","Yazid bin Sharik narrates: I inquired from Sayyiduna Umar (RA) about the issue of reciting in the imitation of the Imam, so he instructed me to recite it. The narrator says: I said: Even if you are the imam (I will recite the recitation), he replied: Even if I am the imam (then you also recite the recitation). (says the narrator) I asked, although you are reciting aloud? He replied, \Even though I am reciting it aloud"
"1212","Abdullah bin Huzail narrates: I asked Sayyiduna Abi bin Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him) should I recite the Qur'aat behind the Imam? He replied yes."
"1213","Sayyidna Mahmud bin Rabi Ansari, who was staying in Ilya, narrates this on the authority of Sayyidna Ubadah bin Samit. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) offered the morning prayer. Do it because whoever does not recite it, his prayer is not (perfect). This document is \Hasan\""."""
"1214","This same hadith has been narrated along with some other isnads in the same way, but it has these words: (It seems) as if you are reciting behind me."
"1215","This tradition is also narrated along with some other isnads."
"1216","This same hadith has been narrated along with some other isnads, but it contains these words: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, \I think you people are reciting behind your Imam when he is reciting loudly. We said"
"1217","Sayyidna Nafi bin Mahmud narrates: Once Sayyidna Ubadah bin Samit (may Allah be pleased with him) arrived late for the morning prayer. Sayyiduna Ubadah also started reciting Surah Fatiha. (The narrator says) When he finished the prayer, I said to Sayyiduna Ubadah (RA): You have done an act about which I could not understand whether it is a Sunnah. Or did you do it by mistake? Sayyiduna Obada inquired which one he is? The narrator says, I heard you reciting Surah Al-Fatihah while Abu Na'im was reciting loudly, then Hazrat Ubada (RA) said, \That is the case. The Prophet (PBUH) led us a prayer in which it was recited loudly"
"1218","Abu Nu'im narrates that Sayyidna Ubada bin Samit (RA) has quoted the saying of the Holy Prophet (SAW) Do you people recite Qur'aat with me while praying? We said yes. The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, \Do it. Yes"
"1219","Sayyidna Ubada bin Samit (RA) narrates that the Prophet (PBUH) asked us, do you people recite with me while I am praying? We said, \We recite Qur'at"
"1220","Nafi bin Mahmud narrates that he heard Sayyidna Ubadah bin Samit (may Allah be pleased with him) reciting Surah Al-Fatiha (in congregational prayer), Abu Naeem (Imam Sahib) was reciting loudly. Nafee said: I heard you reciting Surah Al-Fatiha, although the Imam was reciting loudly, then Sayyiduna Ubadah said: Yes, the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us a prayer in which he recited aloud. We said, \Yes"
"1221","Nafee bin Mahmud narrates: I attended the service of Sayyidna Ubada bin Samit (RA) and he mentioned this hadith regarding the Prophet (PBUH) (followed by the previous hadith), however, it contains these words (the Prophet (PBUH) said). \None of you should ever recite Qur'at"
"1222","Sayyidna Mahmud bin Rabi' Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: Sayyidna Ubadah bin Samit (may Allah be pleased with him) came and stood with me and started reciting following the lead of the Imam, although the Imam was also reciting when he finished praying, I said to him: Abu Waleed! He also started reciting even though he was listening to the imam reciting loudly, then Sayyiduna Ubada bin Samit said, \Yes"
"1223","Amr bin Shuaib narrates this statement of his grandfather in reference to his father. The Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, said that whoever is performing the obligatory prayer, he should recite Surah Al-Fatihah in it and recite one surah along with it. If he does not do this, his prayer will be incomplete and will not be fulfilled. A narrator named Muhammad bin Abdullah bin Ubayd bin Umayr is weak."
"1224","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) instructed him to go and announce among the people that prayer does not begin until Surah Fatiha is recited and no more is recited."
"1225","Muhammad bin Rabi' narrated that Sayyidna Ubadah bin Samit (RA) narrated that the Prophet (PBUH) said that the person who does not recite Surah Al-Fatihah does not pray. A narrator named Ziyad has quoted these words: the prayer of a person who does not recite Surah Fatiha in it is not valid."
"1226","Sayyidna Mahmud bin Rabi', Sayyidna Ubadah bin Samit (RA) narrates that the Prophet (PBUH) said, \There is no prayer for a person who does not recite Fatiha while sleeping.\"" (This tradition is also authentic) The same tradition has been narrated by some other narrators."""
"1227","Sayyiduna Jabir bin Abdullah (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said, “The Imam is the guarantor of what he does, so you also do it.” Sheikh Abu Hatim has stated this. This tradition corrects the position of those who are convinced that recitation will be recited under the guidance of the Imam."
"1228","Mahmood bin Rabi', quoting Sayyidna Ubadah bin Samit, quotes the saying of the Holy Prophet, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, Surah Fatiha is a substitute for all other surahs, but no other surah can replace it. A narrator named Muhammad bin Khalad is unique in narrating this tradition."
"1229","Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) used to give this instruction (the narrator has doubts, perhaps these are the words) He used to say, following the example of the Imam, recite Surah Fatiha and any surah along with it in the first two rak'ahs, and recite only Surah Fatiha in the last two rak'ahs."
"1230","It has been narrated about Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) that he used to order (the narrator is doubtful, perhaps these are the words) He liked that in the first two rak'ahs of Zuhr and Asr, Surah Fatiha and a surah along with it should be recited, and in the last two rak'ats, only Surah Fatiha should be recited, when a person is praying under the guidance of the Imam. The evidence of this tradition is authentic."
"1231","This tradition is narrated along with another document."
"1232","Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) says that in the first two rak'ahs of Zuhr and Asr, recite Surah Al-Fatiha and any surah along with it in the lead of the imam."
"1233","Imam Abu Hanifa (may Allah have mercy on him) quotes this statement of Sayyiduna Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding his chain of transmission. This hadith has been narrated from a narrator named Musa only by Imam Abu Hanifah and a narrator named Hasan and both of them are weak."
"1234","Imam Abu Hanifah (may Allah have mercy on him) narrates this statement of Sayyiduna Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) with regard to his chain of transmission: Once the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in prayer. Argument broke out between the two of them. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to know about this. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, \Whoever is praying in the lead of the Imam"
"1235","Layth bin Saad narrates on the authority of Imam Abu Yusuf and Imam Abu Hanifah, Sayyiduna Jabir bin Abdullah (RA) narrates: A man started reciting Surah Al-Ala following the example of the Prophet (PBUH), when the Prophet (PBUH) finished praying, he asked who among you had recited Surah Al-Ala. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, \I realized that one of you is causing trouble for me.\"""""
"1236","Sayyidna Jabir bin Abdullah (RA) narrates: A man recited the Zuhr and Asr prayers following the example of the Prophet (PBUH), another person stopped him with a gesture, and when he finished the prayer, the first person said: Are you preventing me from reciting the prayer following the Prophet (PBUH)? He said, \Whoever is praying in the lead of the Imam"
"1237","The same hadith has been narrated with reference to Sayyidna Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) along with another chain of transmission. This tradition is also transmitted along with some other isnads. In some isnads, this tradition is transmitted as Mursal Hadith and this is correct."
"1238","Salim bin Abdullah narrates this statement of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with reference to his father (Sayyidna Abdullah bin Umar), whoever is the imam (i.e. performing the prayer in congregation) then the imam’s recitation will count as that person’s recitation. The narrator of this tradition is Muhammad bin Fazl."
"1239","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira Radiyallahu Anhu narrated about this verse here: \And when the Qur'an is recited"
"1240","Sayyidna Imran bin Hussain (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was teaching the people to pray. These words of the tradition have been narrated by the narrator named Hajjaj, other students of Qatadah namely Shuba, Saeed, and others have narrated the tradition on the contrary, they have not narrated these words in their tradition. \The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade them to recite it.\"" Hajj?? The named narrator is not authentic."""
"1241","Sayyiduna Jabir Ibn Abdullah Radiyallahu Anhu narrates: The Holy Prophet (Sallallahu Alaihi Wasallam) has said that the prayer in which Surah Al-Fatiha is not recited is defective, except for the prayer which is led by the Imam. The narrator named Yahya bin Salam is weak. It is true that this hadith is suspended."
"1242","This hadith has been transmitted as a suspended tradition along with another Sunad."
"1243","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said this: The Imam has been appointed so that he is followed."
"1244","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Imam has been appointed so that he may be followed. Imam Abu Abd al-Rahman has stated this, Mukhrami says, this person is Taqqa, that is, the narrator named Muhammad bin Saad (Taqaq)."
"1245","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates this saying of the Prophet (PBUH): The Imam has been appointed so that he may be followed. Do not disagree with him. When he says Takbeer, you also say Takbeer. And when he recites, you remain silent. All of you should also sit and pray. The narrator of this hadith is Ismail bin Aban Zaeef."
"1246","This hadith along with another chain of transmission has been narrated from the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the authority of Sayyidina Abu Huraira (RA). The narrator of this tradition, Abu Saad al-Saghani, is weak."
"1247","Imam Sha'bi narrates that the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that recitation will not be recited following the example of the Imam. This tradition is transmitted."
"1248","Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: A person came to the service of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked, \Should I recite the Qur'aat following the lead of the Imam"
"1249","Hattan bin Abdullah al-Raqashi narrates that once Sayyiduna Abu Musa al-Ash'ari (may Allah be pleased with him) taught us the prayer. Sayyiduna Abu Musa told us: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to teach us the prayer, he (may God bless him and grant him peace) used to teach us. Shaykh Abu Hamid has spelled out this tradition in a similar way to us, the narrator of this tradition, Salim bin Nuh, is not authentic."
"1250","Hattan bin Abdullah al-Raqashi narrates: We performed the Isha prayer following the example of Sayyiduna Abu Musa al-Ash'ari (may God be pleased with him), after which he mentioned a long hadith in which these words are: The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave us a sermon, he taught us how to pray and explained his Sunnah before us. The same hadith has been narrated with reference to some other isnads, but some narrators have quoted these words in it: \And when he recites"
"1251","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said this: When Imam Ghair al-Maghzoob Alaihim Wala Al-Dhaaleen recites, you should remain silent."
"1252","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates this saying of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The narrator of this tradition, Asim, is not authentic: quoting this tradition as a marfu' hadith is delusional."
"1253","Sayyiduna Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said this: Whoever has an imam (i.e., one who is praying in congregation), the recitation of the imam will count as the recitation of that person."
"1254","This tradition is narrated along with another document."
"1255","Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) says that whoever recites after the imam has violated the Sunnah."
"1256","This tradition is also narrated along with another document, but its document is not authentic."
"1257","Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) says: The person who recites following the lead of the Imam has violated the Sunnah."
"1258","Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) says: The person who recites following the lead of the Imam has violated the Sunnah."
"1259","Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) says that a person who does not follow the Sunnah will recite in the example of the Imam."
"1260","Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) says: Reciting the Imam's recitation is sufficient for you."
"1261","The same hadith has been narrated along with another chain of transmission on the authority of Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1262","Sayyiduna Abu Dardaa (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) was asked: Will recitation be recited in every prayer? He said: Yes! So one of the Ansar said: So it became obligatory (says the narrator). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed me and said, because I was sitting closest to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among those present. The narrator has narrated it in the same way, but the narrator of Zaydnam is delusional in it (the correct form of the previous tradition is this)."
"1263","The same hadith is quoted with reference to another chain of transmission in which these words are said: Sayyiduna Abu Darda said, O Kathir: I think that reciting the Imam's recitation will be sufficient for all of them."
"1264","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) has said this: The recitation of the imam will count as the recitation of the person who has an imam. Both Abu Yahya Taymi and Muhammad bin Ubadiya are weak."
"1265","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) offered a prayer and when he finished it, he asked if any of you had recited the Qur’an with me. One of the people present said: I, O Messenger of Allah! The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, \I was also thinking who is competing with me in recitation. When I am reciting in a low voice"
"1266","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas (RA) narrates the saying of the Holy Prophet (SAW): It is sufficient for you to recite the Imam's recitation, whether he recites in a low voice or in a loud voice. A narrator named Abu Musa narrates, I asked Imam Ahmad bin Hanbal about this hadeeth about the Qur'aat transmitted from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas, and he said, \This hadeeth is denied.\"""""
"1267","Sayyiduna Wail bin Hajar (RA) narrates: I heard the Prophet (PBUH) say this: When the Imam recites \Ghira al-Maghdoob 'Alihim Wala Al-Dhaaleen\"" he should say Amen and draw the voice in it. Narrator Abu Bakr-Nami states that this is a Sunnah. The people of Kufa are unique in quoting this tradition. This tradition is authentic and the tradition after it is also authentic."""
"1268","Sayyiduna Wail bin Hajar (RA) narrates: He heard the Prophet (PBUH) say Amen loudly when he recited \Ghira al-Maghdoob (Alaihi Hum Wala Al-Dhaaleen)\""."""
"1269","Sayyiduna Wail bin Hajar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: I recited the curse of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). One of the narrators of this tradition, Abd al-Rahman, says: What is more serious about this is that a person (another narrator of this tradition) inquired about it from Sufyan, so I think that Sufyan spoke about some part of it and this person spoke about another part."
"1270","Sayyiduna Wail bin Hajar (RA) narrates: I prayed following the example of the Holy Prophet (SAW). A narrator named Shuba has narrated these words: The Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, said Amen to the earth."
"1271","Sayyiduna Wail bin Hajar (RA) narrates: I prayed following the example of the Prophet (PBUH) when the Prophet (PBUH) recited Wala al-Dhaaleen, the Prophet (PBUH) said Amen: and the Prophet (PBUH) raised his voice in it. This hadith is authentic."
"1272","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: When the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited Wala al-Zaaleen, he would say Amen and say it loudly."
"1273","This same hadith along with another chain of transmission has been narrated from the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the authority of Sayyidina Abu Huraira. The narrator of the previous tradition, Bahr, is weak in trust."
"1274","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: When the Prophet (PBUH) finished reciting Surah Al-Fatiha, he used to say Amen in a loud voice."
"1275","Sayyiduna Samrah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: I remember this about the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he used to be silent twice during the prayer, one when the imam had said Takbeer and was about to start the recitation, and the second was when he finished reciting Surah Fatiha. Sayyidna Imran bin Hussain (RA) did not accept this, so these people wrote a letter about it to Sayyidna Abi Bin Ka'b (RA) in Madinah, and he confirmed the statement of Sayyidna Samura (RA). Hasan al-Basri heard a hadith from Sayyiduna Samrah (may God be pleased with him) that has been disputed."
"1276","Hasan al-Basri narrates: When Sayyiduna Samrah (RA) used to start the prayer, he would remain silent for a while, then when he finished reciting Wala al-Dhaaleen, he would remain silent for a while."
"1277","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said the Takbir at the beginning of the prayer, he would remain silent for a while. I said: O Messenger of Allah: May my parents be sacrificed to you. Then the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I read this: O Allah! So create a distance between me and my sins as much as You have placed a distance between the East and the West and O Allah cleanse me from my sins as a white cloth is cleansed from dirt."
"1278","Sayyidna Abu Saeed Al-Khudri (RA) narrates: We estimated the time of the Holy Prophet (PBUH) in the Zuhr and Asr prayers, so we estimated that the time of the Prophet (PBUH) in the Zuhr Prayer is as long as thirty verses can be recited in a while, i.e. the length of Surah Sajdah. In the same way, we estimated his stay in the first rak'at of Asr, and it was equal to the last two rak'ats of Zuhr, and we estimated his stay in the last two rak'ats of Asr, and it was half of that. This tradition is proven and correct."
"1279","Qais bin Abu Hazim narrates: I prayed in Basra following Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas, he recited Surah Fatiha in the first rak'ah and recited the first verse of Surah Baqarah, then he stood up for the second rak'ah and recited Surah Fatihah and recited the second verse of Surah Baqarah. He said: \Recite as much of it as you can easily read.\"" The authenticity of this hadeeth is good"
"1280","Sayyiduna Abu Dardaa (RA) narrates: A person stood up and asked: O Messenger of Allah: Will it be recited in every rak'ah? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, one of the people present said: It has become necessary. (The narrator of this hadith) Sayyiduna Abu Darda said (to his disciple) O Katheer: (Many say: I was by his side at that time) I think that the recitation of the Imam when he was leading the people in prayer would be sufficient for those people. The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said this: I think that reciting the Imam's recitation will be sufficient for these people. The narrator is delusional in this hadith. The truth is: These words are from Sayyiduna Abu Darda' as narrated by a narrator named Ibn Wahhib, Allah knows best."
"1281","Sayyiduna Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught us how to pray. The narrator says) When Sayyidna Sa'd (RA) found out about this tradition, he said: My brother (Sayyidna Abdullah Bin Mas'ud (RA) said correctly: Yes, we used to do this before, but then we were ordered to do it this way, then Sayyidna Sa'd (RA) placed both hands on the knees (this means: ordered to do this while bowing)."
"1282","The same hadith is narrated along with another document, however, it contains these words: He said Takbeer, raised both hands, then when he went into Ruku, he put both hands together and placed them between both knees. When Sayyidna Saad (RA) came to know about this, he said: My brother said right: Yes, we used to do it like this before, but then we were ordered to place both palms on both knees. This certificate is proven and correct."
"1283","Alqamah bin Wail narrates this statement of his father (Sayyidna Wail bin Hajar, may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to spread his fingers when he bowed, and when he went into prostration, he used to bring all five fingers together. This tradition is also narrated along with some other isnads."
"1284","Abdullah bin Burida narrates this statement of his father: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Burida! When you bow down, recite this: \Hear Allah"
"1285","Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: When we used to pray in the footsteps of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he used to recite, Sama’ullah al-ulman Hamda, and the person behind the Prophet (peace be upon him) also used to recite, Sama’Allah al-man Hamda."
"1286","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said this: When the imam recites Sama'Allah Al-Man Hamdah, the person behind him should recite, 'Allahum Rabbanalik Walik Al-Hamdah.'"
"1287","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) led us in prayer, then he turned towards us and said: Do you people recite after the imam? We said: There is a gentleman among us who recited this."
"1288","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led his companions in prayer. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished praying, he turned to them and said: Were you reciting while the Imam was reciting? The people remained silent. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said this three times, and a gentleman said: A few gentlemen have stated this: We have done the same. These words belong to a narrator named Farsi."
"1289","This tradition is narrated along with another document."
"1290","Sayyiduna Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to those present who were reciting aloud (following the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)): You have made it difficult for me to recite. (Sayyidna Abdullah Radiyallahu Anhu narrates:) Earlier we (used to reply to salams) in prayer, then we were told: Prayer is a distraction (therefore no conversation or speech should be made between prayers)."
"1291","Saeed bin Musayyib used to say here: When you find some people in the state of ruku, say Takbeer and go into Ruku, then that one Takbeer will be sufficient for you as Takbeer Tahreema and Takbeer going into Ruku. This has also been narrated from Saeed bin Musaib: He says: Whoever forgets to say Takbeer Tahreema at the beginning of the prayer, then he says Takbeer to go into Ruku, then doing so will be sufficient for him."
"1292","Sayyiduna Huzaifah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite Subhan Rabi al-Azeem wa Bhamdah three times while bowing and while prostrating, he used to recite Subhan Rabi al-Ala: three times."
"1293","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Masoud (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: This is a Sunnah: a person should read Subhan Rabi al-Azeem wa Bhamda while bowing, and Subhan Rabi al-Ala wa Bhamda while prostrating."
"1294","Sayyiduna Ali bin Abu Talib (RA) narrates: When the Prophet (PBUH) went into prostration in the obligatory prayer, he used to recite the following: \O Allah"
"1295","It has been narrated in another chain of transmission, which has these words: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to bow down, it specifically mentions bowing and does not mention other things."
"1296","Sayyiduna Jubayr bin Muta'im (RA) narrates: When the Prophet (PBUH) went into Ruku, he would recite Subhan Rabi Al-Azeem three times."
"1297","Ubaidullah bin Abdullah narrates this statement from his father: I remember this about the Holy Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace: He used to recite Subhan Rabi al-Azeem three times while bowing."
"1298","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said: When a person bows, he should recite the Tasbeeh three times, because he will be reciting the Tasbeeh with his body, and with him three hundred and thirty-three bones and three hundred and thirty-three veins are reciting the Tasbeeh of Allah."
"1299","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Masoud (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said this: When a person recites Subhan Rabi al-Azeem three times while bowing, then his bowing will be complete."
"1300","Sayyida Aisha Siddiqa (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) used to recite this dua while bowing:"
"1301","The narrator says: I inquired from Sulaiman bin Harb: Shu'ba narrated this: Hisham narrated this tradition to me, he said: He narrated this."
"1302","Sayyida Ayesha Siddiqa says: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went into prostration, his fingers would point towards the Qiblah."
"1303","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went into prostration, he would place both hands on his knees."
"1304","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a person goes into prostration, he should place both his hands before his legs and he should not sit like a camel."
"1305","It is narrated along with another chain of transmission: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a person goes into prostration, he should place his hands in front of his knees and should not sit like a camel."
"1306","There is this addition in another hadith: When the imam says Sama'Allah Al-Man Hamdah, the person behind him should read: Sama'Allah Liman Hamdah Al-Hum Rabnalik Al-HamdAllah Ta'ala heard the praise of the person who recited his praise, O Allah! O our Lord! All praise is reserved for You."
"1307","Sayyiduna Wail bin Hajar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went into prostration, he would place both his knees in front of his hands, and when he stood up, he would raise both hands before his knees. Narrator Yazid is unique in narrating this tradition, he narrated it from a participant and this narrator is not authentic."
"1308","Sayyiduna Anas (RA) narrates: I remember this about the Holy Prophet (SAW): The Prophet (SAW) said the Takbeer until he raised both his thumbs to his ears, then he bowed until every joint of the Prophet’s (SAW) body stayed in its place, then he raised his blessed head until every joint of his body stayed in its place, then he bowed while saying the Takbeer, then he bowed both his knees. Put the hands on the ground first. Al-Ala bin Ismail is also unique in quoting this hadith, Allah knows best."
"1309","Abu Qalaba narrates: Sayyiduna Abu Sulaiman Malik bin Hawairat (RA) came to our mosque, he said: By Allah! I pray you, I do not intend to pray at this time, but I want to show you how I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying. The narrator narrates that when he got up after performing the second prostration in the first rak'at, he sat down first (then stood up)."
"1310","Sayyiduna Malik bin Hawairat Laithi (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: I remember this about the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): He (peace be upon him) was praying, when he was in the first and third rak'ahs, he did not get up until he first went straight. This hadith is authentic."
"1311","Sayyiduna Malik bin Hawairat (RA) narrates: Those people came to the service of the Prophet (PBUH) along with one of his companions. The Prophet (PBUH) said to both of them: When the time for prayer comes, both of you should call the Adhaan, both of you should say Iqamah, and let the one of you who is older than the two of you lead the prayer, and you should pray in the same way as you have seen me praying. This tradition is authentic."
"1312","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said this: If you want your prayer to be pure, you should lead your righteous people, (i.e. make them Imams)."
"1313","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said this: He who observes one rak'at of prayer has observed this prayer, that is, he finds it before the imam has raised his back (from the bowing of the last rak'at)."
"1314","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said: When you come for prayer and we are in the state of prostration, you should also go into prostration, but do not count it as a rak'ah."
"1315","Sayyiduna Abu Mas'ud (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said: The person who does not keep his back straight in bowing and prostrating does not pray. The chain of this hadith is proven and correct."
"1316","This tradition is also narrated along with another chain of transmission, but it has these words: \The prayer of a person who does not keep his back upright in prayer is not performed.\"""""
"1317","Sayyida Aisha Siddiqa (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) saw one of his wives who was praying, she was not putting her nose on the ground. The Prophet (PBUH) said: O woman! You should put your nose on the ground. The person who does not put his nose and forehead on the ground during the prayer does not pray. The narrator of this hadith is weak, and this tradition is not authentic regarding Muqatil's narration regarding Arwa."
"1318","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas (RA) narrates the saying of the Prophet (PBUH): \There is no prayer for a person who does not put his nose on the ground.\"" Other narrators have narrated it as Mursal Hadith."""
"1319","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) saw a person praying who was not placing his nose on the ground. This hadith has been transmitted as a messenger along with another sanad."
"1320","Abdul Aziz narrates I said to Wahib: O Abu Na'im! Why don't you keep your forehead and nose on the ground during prayer? So he said: I heard Sayyidna Jabir bin Abdullah narrating this: I saw the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrating on the upper part of the forehead, i.e. where the hair begins. Narrator Abdul Aziz is unique in narrating this tradition and it is not authentic."
"1321","Sayyidna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Sunnah way (of sitting) in prayer is to put the left foot down and keep the right foot standing."
"1322","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) says: The way (to sit) in prayer is to spread the left foot and raise the right foot."
"1323","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) says: The Sunnah way (of sitting) in prayer is that you should put your left foot down and raise your right foot. All these hadiths are authentic, but they have been narrated only by a narrator named Thaqafi."
"1324","On the authority of Amir bin Abdullah, his father (Sayyidna Abdullah bin Zubair, may God be pleased with him) narrated this statement: When the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, used to pray (during the prayer), he used to pray (says the narrator) that is, he used to pray in tashahhud. He used to hold his left knee with his blessed hand."
"1325","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) used to teach us the words of tashahhud in the same way that the Prophet (PBUH) used to teach us the Qur’an. \All verbal and physical blessed pure worship is reserved for Allah"
"1326","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) used to teach us this tashahhud (in these words): \All blessed and pure worship is for Allah"
"1327","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Masoud (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: Before tashahhud became obligatory, we used to recite: Peace be upon Allah, Gabriel and Michael. All physical and verbal acts of worship are pure acts of worship reserved for Allah, O Prophet! Peace be upon you, may the mercy and blessings of Allah be upon us, may peace be upon us, the righteous servants of Allah. Its certificate is authentic."
"1328","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Masoud (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to teach us tashahhud in these words: All worship is for Allah (after this the narrator mentioned the previous hadith)."
"1329","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) used to recite the following in Tashahhud: All verbal and physical acts of worship are reserved for Allah, O Prophet! Peace be upon you and may the blessings of Allah be upon you. Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) says: I have added these words to it: And may His blessings also descend (these are the words of the martyrdom of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) May peace be upon us and all the righteous servants of Allah. These are the words of tashahhud) I bear witness that Sayyiduna Muhammad (peace be upon him) is his servant and his messenger."
"1330","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) says: The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to teach tashahhud in these words. \All pure worship is reserved for Allah. Peace be upon you"
"1331","A narrator named Yaqoob bin Ashjaja narrates: Aun bin Abdullah wrote down the words of tashahud narrated by Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, then he held my hand and told: Sayyiduna Umar once held his hand and said this. \Salutations to Allah"
"1332","Hattan bin Abdullah al-Raqashi narrates: These people prayed following the example of Sayyidna Abu Musa Ash'ari, may Allah be pleased with him. Sayyidna Abu Musa Ash'ari said: Once the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) explained to us the way to pray before us while giving a sermon, and he taught us the Sunnah. After this, the narrator has mentioned the entire hadith, which also contains these words: When a person sits in Qa'dah, recite these words: All verbal and physical worship is reserved for Allah, O Prophet! Peace be upon you and may the mercy and blessings of Allah be upon you, may peace be upon us and upon all the righteous servants of Allah. The disciples of the narrator named Qatadah have added these words to the names: He is One and He has no partner, while other narrators have narrated the opposite regarding Qatadah."
"1333","A narrator named Qasim narrates: Once Al-Qamah held my hand and said: Once Sayyiduna Abdullah held my hand and told me that: Once the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, held my hand and taught me the words of tashahhud during prayer (these are the words). All verbal and physical worship is reserved for Allah, O Prophet! Peace be upon you and may the blessings and mercy of Allah be upon us and peace be upon us and all the righteous servants of Allah. Other narrators have followed in narrating it."
"1334","The same hadith is also narrated along with another chain of narrators. Some narrators have quoted additional words at the end of it. \When you read this (the narrator doubts maybe these are the words) you will do so"
"1335","A narrator named Qasim narrates: Al-Qamah held my hand and said: Once Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Masoud held my hand and said: Once the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, held my hand and taught me the words of tashahhud (these are the words): All verbal and physical worship is reserved for Allah, O Prophet! May peace be upon you and may the blessings and mercy of Allah be upon us and may peace be upon us and all the righteous servants of Allah. Sayyidna Abdullah bin Masoud says: When you read this, you have performed the obligatory prayer. Now if you want to get up, get up. If you want to sit, sit down. The narrator of this tradition here is Shababa Thaqa. He has mentioned this separately at the end of the tradition. Some other narrators have also narrated it in the same way as the words of Sayyiduna Abdullah, they have not narrated (the additional words) as a marfu' hadith."
"1336","A narrator named Qasim narrates: Once Alqamah held my hand and told him. All verbal and physical worship is reserved for Allah, O Prophet! May peace be upon you and may the blessings and mercy of Allah be upon us and may peace be upon us and all the righteous servants of Allah. Then Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Masoud said: When you have completed this (the narrator doubts perhaps these are the words), when you have done this, you have performed your prayer."
"1337","The same tradition is also narrated along with some other isnads in which there are these words: A narrator named Qasim narrates: Alqamah held my hand and (there are also these words in the narration) Sayyidna Abdullah bin Masoud (may Allah be pleased with him) held the hand of Alqamah and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) held the hand of Sayyidna Abdullah bin Masoud (may Allah be pleased with him) and taught him the words of tashahhud (which are these). All verbal and physical worship is reserved for Allah, O Prophet! May peace be upon you and may the blessings and mercy of Allah be upon us and may peace be upon us and all the righteous servants of Allah. Then Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Masoud Radiyallahu Anhu said this: When you are finished with this, you will finish the prayer."
"1338","Mujahid narrates: Ibn Abi Layli (the narrator is in doubt, perhaps these are the words) Abu Muammar has narrated this, Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Masoud Radiyallahu Anhu taught me the words of tashahhud and narrated that these words were taught to me by the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allaah be upon him) in the same way that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allaah be upon him) used to teach us a chapter of the Qur’an (these are the words): All pure and physical and verbal worship is reserved for Allah, O Prophet! Peace be upon you and may the blessings and mercy of Allah be upon us and may peace be upon us and all the good servants of Allah. So send blessings on Sayyiduna Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and on his family, just as You sent blessings on Sayyiduna Ibrahim, indeed, to the Lord of praise and greatness, O Allah, send blessings on us along with them. May the blessings of the people of faith be upon Sayyidna Muhammad (peace be upon him), who is a prophet, and may peace be upon him, may the mercy and blessings of Allah be upon him. Mujahid says that when a person uses these words while sending greetings, and greetings are sent to all the righteous servants of Allah, then he has sent greetings to all the people living in the heavens and the earth. A narrator named Abdul Wahab bin Mujahid is weak."
"1339","A narrator named Ibn Ishaq narrated this: He narrated to me this hadith about sending blessings on the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in prayer: When a Muslim person sends blessings on the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) during prayer. (There are these words in a hadith) Sayyiduna Abu Mas'ud Ansari (RA) narrates: A person came and sat in front of the Holy Prophet (SAW). We were around him at that time. This person said, O Messenger of Allah, we have become familiar with the way of sending salutations on you while we are praying, so how do we send salutations on you while we are praying? The narrator narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained silent until we wished that this person had not asked him this question, but then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you wish to send blessings upon me, recite this: \O Allah! So send blessings upon Sayyiduna Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)"
"1340","Abdullah bin Burida narrated this statement of his father: The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Burida! When you sit in the tashahhud in prayer, never give up the words of tashahhud and sending blessings on me, because this is the zakat of prayer, and also send greetings to all the prophets and messengers of Allah, and send greetings to all the righteous servants of Allah."
"1341","Sayyida Aisha Siddiqa (RA) narrates: I heard the Prophet (PBUH) saying: Prayer is not accepted without performing ablution and sending blessings upon me. Amr bin Shamar and Jabir Jafi are both weak narrators."
"1342","Sayyiduna Sahl bin Sa'd (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said: \There is no prayer for a person who does not send blessings on his Prophet.\"" One of the narrators of this tradition"
"1343","Sayyidna Abu Mas'ud Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that whoever prays and does not send blessings on me and my family in it, his prayer will not be accepted. One of the narrators of this hadith is Jabir Zaeef, and it has been disputed in narrating the hadith from him."
"1344","Sayyiduna Abu Mas'ud Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: If I do not send blessings on the family of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while praying, then I consider that my prayer has not been completed."
"1345","Sayyidna Abu Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: During the prayer during which I do not send blessings upon Sayyidna Muhammad (peace be upon him), I think that my prayer has not been completed."
"1346","Amir bin Sa'd on the authority of his father (Sayyidna Sa'd bin Abi Waqqas may Allah be pleased with him) narrates about the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): He used to salute the right until the whiteness of his blessed face was visible and he used to salute the left until the whiteness of his blessed face was seen. The evidence of this tradition is authentic."
"1347","Sayyidna Ammar bin Yasir (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: When the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to greet the right side, the whiteness of his right cheek would be seen, and when he greeted him to the left, the whiteness of his left cheek would be seen."
"1348","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Masoud (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say salam to the right, he used to say Salam alaikum wa rahmatullah, until the whiteness of his face was visible, and when he used to say salam to the left (the same thing happened). Narrator Abu Ishaq has been disputed in narrating this tradition, the second chain of this tradition is better."
"1349","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Masoud (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: I remember this very well about the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he used to say Takbeer every time he stood up, bowed, stood up, and sat down, and he used to say Salaam alaikum wa rahmatullah, salam alaikum wa rahmatullah, until the whiteness of his blessed face could be seen. (Sayyidna Abdullah says) I have seen Sayyidna Abu Bakr and Sayyidna Umar also do the same."
"1350","Sayyidna Bara bin Azib (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to greet him twice (i.e. on both sides)."
"1351","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Masoud (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: I may forget whatever I want, but I will not forget the way the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say salam during prayer. After that, Sayyiduna Abdullah Radiyallahu Anhu said this: The sight of the whiteness of both the faces of the Holy Prophet, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, seems to be in my eyes even today."
"1352","Sayyida Aisha Sadiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to make a salam in prayer and he would also turn towards the front and say (the words of salutation): He used to lean slightly to the right."
"1353","Sayyidna Samura bin Jundab (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) used to say salam once while facing the front in prayer, but when he turned his salam to the right, he also turned to the side of repentance."
"1354","Abd al-Muhaiman narrates this statement of his grandfather regarding his father: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to make a salam to the right in prayer."
"1355","Abd al-Muhaiman narrates this statement of his grandfather regarding his father: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say one salam, but the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not say any more salams."
"1356","Sayyidna Abu Saeed Al-Khudri (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) said: The key to prayer is ablution. A narrator has copied the word tahoor instead of the word ablution."
"1357","Sayyiduna Samrah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us: We should send greetings to our rulers and send greetings to each other."
"1358","Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: When Najm is sitting in the amount of tashahhud, his prayer is complete."
"1359","Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The key to prayer is ablution."
"1360","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Zayd (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates the saying of the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Wudu is performed before prayer, it begins with Takbeer and ends with salutation."
"1361","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: Once the Holy Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to pray, and when he said the Takbeer, he turned back from there. I was in a state of janabat and I did not remember to take bath."
"1362","Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) started the prayer, he (PBUH) said the Takbeer, following the example of the Prophet (PBUH), we also recited the Takbeer, then the Prophet (PBUH) signaled to those present that you should remain in the state you are in. The drops were dripping. A narrator named Abdul Wahab has narrated the opposite tradition."
"1363","Sayyidna Bakr bin Abdullah Muzni (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) started the prayer, he said the Takbeer and the people behind him also said the Takbeer, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) broke the prayer and signaled to his Companions to remain in the same state as they were, so they remained in the state of standing, until when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned, drops of water were dripping from his head. A narrator named Abdul Wahab narrates: We also issue a fatwa accordingly."
"1364","Sayyiduna Wabsa narrates: He stood alone at the back of the row and prayed, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to pray again."
"1365","Sayyiduna Wabsa (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: A man prayed standing alone at the back of the row, and the Prophet ordered him to pray again."
"1366","Sayyiduna Bara bin Azib (RA) narrates: The Prophet (PBUH) led the people in prayer. He was not in the state of ablution at that time. The prayer of the people was completed and the Prophet (PBUH) repeated this prayer."
"1367","The same hadith is also narrated along with another chain of transmission, but it has these words of yours: When the imam leads the prayer to the people who are in a state of ablution due to forgetfulness, then the prayer of the people will be valid and the imam will perform this prayer again."
"1368","Sayyiduna Bara bin Azib (RA) narrates the saying of the Holy Prophet (SAW): If an imam forgets to lead people in a state of janabat, their prayer will be valid, then this imam should perform ablution and repeat his prayer. A narrator named Isa bin Ibrahim narrated these same words."
"1369","Saeed bin Musaib narrates: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led the people in the state of Janabat (forgetfulness). The Prophet (peace be upon him) also repeated his prayer and the people also repeated their prayer. This tradition is transmitted and the narrator named Abu Jabar is an obsolete hadith."
"1370","Asim bin Zamra narrates this about Sayyidna Ali: Once he led the people in prayer in the state of Janabat (forgetfulness), then Sayyidna Ali repeated the prayer and instructed the people to repeat the prayer. The narrator of this tradition is Amr bin Khalid, this person is Abu Khalid Wasti (who has quoted the Musnad of Imam Zayd regarding Imam Zayd)."
"1371","Shareed Thaqafi narrates: Once Sayyiduna Umar (R.A.) taught the prayer to the people. They were in a state of janabat. They repeated that prayer, but he did not order the people to repeat that prayer."
"1372","Muhammad Bin Amr narrates: Once Sayyidna Uthman Ghani (RA) led the people in prayer, he was in a state of janabat at that time, and later when he saw a mark of ihtilam on his clothes, he said: I am old, by Allah, I think I have contracted janabat, but I did not know about it, then Hazrat Uthman repeated the prayer, and of course he did not order the people to perform that prayer again. A narrator named Abd al-Rahman narrates: It is agreed that a person with Janabat will repeat this prayer, but people will not repeat this prayer. As far as I know, there is no disagreement about this."
"1373","Salim narrates the saying of his father (Sayyidna Abdullah bin Umar, may Allah be pleased with him) about a person who leads people in the state of ablution."
"1374","Narrated by Nafi: Once Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) prayed to his companions, later he remembered that he had touched his private parts (due to which his ablution was broken), so Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) performed ablution again (and prayed again), but he did not instruct those people to perform that prayer again. Ibn Mahdi narrates: I inquired from Sufyan: Are you aware of this, that a narrator has also narrated this? These people also prayed again? He replied no, only a narrator named Hamad has narrated this."
"1375","Sayyidna Jabir bin Samrah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: We performed an obligatory prayer following the example of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rubbed his hands together during the prayer. I felt the coolness of his tongue on my hands, By Allah, if my brother Sulaiman (peace be upon him) had not prayed this before me, I would have tied this devil to a pillar of this mosque and the children of Madinah would have circled around him (and mocked him)."
